// Kiddo School — EXTRA CLASS · Home and Daily Life: /extra-classes/home-and-
// daily-life/learn/ lessons. One step-by-step teaching lesson per card, in the
// set's tour order (rooms → routines → kitchen → chores → around → sounds →
// safe and green). The original flashcard sits LEFT with the drawing canvas;
// this teaching sits RIGHT. Written fresh — the library pages in
// data-home-daily-life.mjs answer WHAT the thing is and HOW it feels; no text
// is shared between the two. The US/UK/CA word pairs (tap/faucet,
// torch/flashlight, wardrobe/closet, trolley/cart, bin/trash, parcel/package)
// are taught in both flavors wherever the card carries them. The lesson `say`
// field is a NEW script, never the library card's `say`.
export const lpContent={
 'rooms-at-home':{
  title:'The Rooms at Home Lesson',one:'Turn your actual house into the answer key for five room names.',
  seoTitle:'Rooms at Home Lesson for Kids — A House Tour in Five Words (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach the rooms of the house with a live tour — bedroom, kitchen, bathroom, living room and garden — until your child names them all.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the five rooms as a tour of your real home: the card is the map, and the house is the territory.',
  what:'A home is divided into rooms, and each room has a name that says what happens there: sleeping, cooking, washing, gathering, growing.',
  mean:'Room names are the first map words a child owns. Once the five are known, every instruction you give — take this to the kitchen — becomes followable, and every question your child asks becomes askable.',
  know:'You need the card and a house — any house. The tour takes five minutes and ends with your child standing in the right room when you call it out.',
  why:'Children learn location words fastest when their legs carry them there. A card on the couch cannot do what a sprint to the bathroom does.',
  dev:'Spatial vocabulary (in, where, which room), category thinking (a house is rooms; a room is things), and the confidence of navigating home territory by name.',
  vocab:'bedroom, kitchen, bathroom, living room, garden, room, house',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five rooms, one house — let us find them all."\n2) Tour the real house in the card order: bedroom, kitchen, bathroom, living room, then out the door to the garden or yard.\n3) In each room, one anchor sentence: "this is where we sleep" — and one object: point to the bed.\n4) Back on the couch, cover the card and ask "which room has the sofa?"\n5) Finish standing in the room your child loves best — the game ends with a win.',
  say:'"Rooms at home — let us take the tour! Where do we sleep? Run there! Where does the food cook? Go! You know every room in this house."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Rooms at Home" sit over five small scenes side by side: a bed in the bedroom, a stove in the kitchen, a sink and shower in the bathroom, a sofa in the living room, and a fenced garden with a tree.',
  ask:'"Which room do we cook in?" "Which room has your bed?" "Where does the soap live?"',
  childSay:'Your child might announce room names at random all day — "bathroom!" — drag the card on the tour like a treasure map, or ask where the rooms are in grandma&rsquo;s house.',
  real:'Every home you visit — a friend&rsquo;s flat, a hotel, a holiday cottage — has the same five rooms wearing the same names. The words pack themselves for every trip.',
  play:'Room Race: call a room, the child runs to it, touches one true thing in it and runs back for the next call. Three rounds and the map is permanent. Grown-up runs the last round — and loses dramatically.',
  check:'Ask: "How many rooms on the card?" (five) "Where do we sleep?" (the bedroom) "What do we call the outside room?" (the garden — or the yard)'},
 'parts-of-a-home-inside':{
  title:'The Parts of the Home Lesson',one:'Give your child the five inside words that every safety instruction is built on.',
  seoTitle:'Parts of the Home Lesson for Kids — Window, Door, Stairs, Wall, Floor (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach the inside parts of a home — window, door, stairs, wall and floor — with a touch-hunt through your own hallway.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the fixed parts of a home by touch: the child finds each part in your real house, and the words attach to the touching.',
  what:'Inside every home are the parts that do not move: windows that let light in, doors that open, stairs that climb, walls that hold, floors that carry.',
  mean:'These five words are the skeleton of every direction and every safety rule a child will hear at home — feet on the floor, hand on the rail, close the door. Owning the words makes the rules sayable.',
  know:'You need the card and one hallway. The touch-hunt needs no props: a window, a door, a wall and a floor are always within reach, and the stairs are wherever they are.',
  why:'A child who can name the parts can also ask about them — "why is the wall cold?" — and every question about a fixed part is a question about how a home works.',
  dev:'Vocabulary for fixed structures, body-safe navigation (stairs with a hand), and the first glimmer that a house is a made thing, built of parts that were chosen.',
  vocab:'window, door, stairs, wall, floor, inside, part',
  teach:'1) Show the card and read the five words slowly, touching each picture.\n2) Stand in the hallway: "find a wall" — hand on it. "Find the floor" — feet on it.\n3) Find a door and open-close it once, gently. Find a window and look through it.\n4) At the stairs: hand on the rail, feet on the floor, slow climb, counting steps aloud.\n5) Back at the card, ask your child to point to the part they touched first.',
  say:'"Parts of the home — touch hunt! Hand on the wall, feet on the floor, one door, one window. The stairs are the slowest part — we take them with a hand."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Parts of the Home" sit over five inside pieces: a blue-framed window, a closed door, a staircase with a rail, a plain wall and a wide floor.',
  ask:'"What does the window do?" (lets light in) "What do we hold at the stairs?" (the rail — our hand on it) "What is under your feet right now?"',
  childSay:'Your child might pat every wall in the house like a drum, announce FLOOR at each room change, or ask why the window cannot open all the way.',
  real:'Offices, libraries and grandparent homes all have the same five parts. The touch-hunt replays itself everywhere you go — and stairs in new places get the careful version automatically.',
  play:'Parts Bingo: draw a tiny five-square grid on paper — window, door, stairs, wall, floor. Walk the house and mark each square as the child finds and touches the real part. Full card, full pride.',
  safety:'Stairs come with their rule attached, said the same way every time: hand on the rail, feet on the floor, one step at a time. The rule and the word grow up together.'},
 'parts-of-a-home-outside':{
  title:'The Outside Parts Lesson',one:'Read a house like a picture, from the curb: door, windows, roof, chimney, garage.',
  seoTitle:'Parts of a Home Lesson for Kids — Reading a House from the Curb (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach the outside parts of a home — front door, roof, chimney and garage — on a standing-still walk around your own street.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the outside of a home as a reading exercise: the house is the book, and the curb is the reading chair.',
  what:'From the outside a home shows the parts that face the world: the front door, the windows, the roof on top, the chimney that used to carry smoke, the garage that shelters the car.',
  mean:'Outside parts are how children learn that homes come in kinds — with garages and without, flat roofs and pointy. "Some homes do, some don&rsquo;t" is the sentence this lesson plants.',
  know:'You need the card and a window or doorstep with a view of any house. No walking required for round one; the street does the showing.',
  why:'House-watching is calm, free and endlessly available. A child who can read a house&rsquo;s parts from a distance has a five-minute game that works in every neighborhood on Earth.',
  dev:'Observation from distance, comparison (this roof, that roof), and honest variation — the same word can fit different-looking homes.',
  vocab:'front door, roof, chimney, garage, window, outside',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "every part on this card is outside — let us spot them."\n2) From the window or step, find your own front door, then your roof.\n3) Hunt for a chimney (some houses have none — say so) and a garage.\n4) Count the windows you can see from where you stand.\n5) Next walk, repeat from the curb — a different house each time.',
  say:'"Parts of a home — outside! There is our front door, there is the roof sitting on top like a hat. Does our house have a chimney? Let us look!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Parts of a Home" sit over a pale-blue front door, a four-pane window, a blue-tiled roof, a brick chimney and a garage with its door down.',
  ask:'"What is on top of the house?" (the roof) "Where does the car sleep?" (the garage — if there is one) "Which part do guests knock on?"',
  childSay:'Your child might declare every roof a hat, ask why some chimneys are quiet, or point out garages for the whole length of the street.',
  real:'House-reading travels: on any drive, any walk, any storybook picture, the five outside parts are findable. Buildings in books get read with the same words.',
  play:'House I-Spy: "I spy with my little eye a part of a home that is red" — the roof, the door, whatever fits. Five finds wins; the winner picks the next house to read.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the roof do?" (sits on top, keeps the rain out) "Where does smoke come from when there is a fire?" (the chimney) "What part do we knock?" (the front door)'},
 'living-room-words':{
  title:'The Living Room Lesson',one:'Name the family room from the best seat in it — the sofa.',
  seoTitle:'Living Room Lesson for Kids — Naming the Family Room, Sofa to Remote (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach living-room words — sofa, television, coffee table, floor lamp, remote control — from a sitting-down name-five game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the living room without anyone standing up: the sofa is the classroom, and everything taught is within pointing distance.',
  what:'The living room is the gathering room: the sofa to sit on, the television to watch, the coffee table to rest a cup, the floor lamp for light, the remote control for the deciding.',
  mean:'Children already own this room — naming its parts makes their favorite territory official. Vocabulary that attaches to comfort sticks faster than vocabulary that attaches to lessons.',
  know:'You need the card and the sofa. Round one is five pointing answers from sitting down; round two is the swap-seats version, which refreshes the answers for free.',
  why:'The living room is where families talk, so it is where family vocabulary pays off daily: put it on the coffee table, sit on the sofa, lamp on for reading.',
  dev:'Object naming in context, positional words (on, under, beside — where IS the remote?), and turn-taking in a seated game.',
  vocab:'sofa, television, coffee table, floor lamp, remote control, living room',
  teach:'1) Sit on the sofa with the card: "this room has five names on it — let us find them from here."\n2) Point at each real thing: sofa (we are on it), television, coffee table, floor lamp, remote.\n3) Swap seats and name five again — the new view finds new details.\n4) Ask: "what does the remote do?" — one button press, together, then it rests.\n5) End with the lamp: switch it on for story time, and the room has a mood you made.',
  say:'"Living room words — from the best seat in the house! Sofa under us, television there, lamp in the corner. Now — where is that remote hiding?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Living Room Words" sit over a blue sofa with cushions, a wide television, a round coffee table, a tall floor lamp and a remote control with many buttons.',
  ask:'"Where do we sit together?" (the sofa) "What rests our cups?" (the coffee table) "Who turned the lamp on just now?"',
  childSay:'Your child might claim the remote seat as a throne, label the whole room aloud to a stuffed audience, or put the remote exactly where the card shows it.',
  real:'Every gathering place has a sofa-word and a screen-word — hotels, waiting rooms, grandparents. The child who names their living room names every living room.',
  play:'Remote Hunt: hide the remote (in plain sight — under the cushion, on the coffee table, beside the lamp) and give warm-and-cold clues. The finder presses one button as the prize. The room teaches itself while you play.',
  check:'Ask: "Where are we sitting?" (the sofa) "What is the little table called?" (the coffee table) "What do we press to choose the show?" (the remote control)'},
 'furniture-words':{
  title:'The Furniture Lesson',one:'Teach the big-word category — furniture — through five things you can sit on, kneel on and open.',
  seoTitle:'Furniture Lesson for Kids — What Makes a Chair Furniture? (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach furniture as a category — armchair, bookshelf, rug, cabinet, stool — with a feel-it tour and a one-job question for each piece.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches furniture as a category word: not five separate facts, but one big idea — the big things that stay put — with five members of the club.',
  what:'Furniture is the family of big, unmoving things in a home: the armchair, the bookshelf, the rug, the cabinet and the stool all belong to it.',
  mean:'Category words are a leap for young minds: sofa is a thing, furniture is a kind of thing. Once the leap lands, it lands everywhere — appliances, tools, clothes — and the world sorts itself.',
  know:'You need the card and five real pieces — the card&rsquo;s five or your home&rsquo;s five. The feel-it part matters: each piece gets touched in the way that is truest to it.',
  why:'Furniture is the gentlest category to start with: every member is big, visible and daily. Success here makes the next category word easy.',
  dev:'Category formation (member vs kind), function thinking (what is this piece FOR), and the comparison habit — how is a stool like a chair, how is it not?',
  vocab:'furniture, armchair, bookshelf, rug, cabinet, stool',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "all five of these belong to one family called furniture."\n2) Visit the real pieces: sit in the armchair, open and shut the cabinet once, stand on the stool beside a grown-up.\n3) Kneel on the rug — "the rug is furniture you can feel with your knees."\n4) Ask the one-job question at each piece: what does the bookshelf do? What does the stool do?\n5) Count the family: how many furniture pieces in this room?',
  say:'"Furniture is the big-word for the things that stay put. The armchair stays, the bookshelf stays — let us visit the whole family and ask each one: what is your job?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Furniture Words" sit over a soft blue armchair, a bookshelf with books inside, a striped rug, a two-door cabinet and a three-legged stool.',
  ask:'"Which furniture holds books?" "Which furniture do we kneel on?" "What is the difference between a chair and a stool?"',
  childSay:'Your child might extend the family eagerly — "is the bed furniture? the dresser?" — kneel on every rug in the house to check it is real furniture, or open the cabinet to greet the dishes.',
  real:'Waiting rooms, schools and libraries all have furniture — the category word works the moment you walk in. Bookshelf is the member your child will meet again first.',
  play:'Furniture Says: like Simon Says, but every command is a true furniture fact — furniture says sit, furniture says kneel, furniture says open, furniture says stand on the stool with a hand. Wrong moves are giggled at, not scored.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the big word for big stay-put things?" (furniture) "Is the sofa furniture?" (yes) "What does the bookshelf carry?" (the books)'},
 'bedroom-words':{
  title:'The Bedroom Words Lesson',one:'Name the five things a good night is made of, in the order they happen.',
  seoTitle:'Bedroom Lesson for Kids — Bed, Pillow, Blanket, Wardrobe, Nightstand (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach bedroom words — bed, pillow, blanket, wardrobe and bedside table — as the bedtime sequence they really are.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bedroom as a sequence in disguise: the five words are the five moves of bedtime, and the child learns them by doing them.',
  what:'The bedroom keeps the bed, the pillow, the blanket, the wardrobe (the closet) and the bedside table — the five things between waking and sleeping.',
  mean:'Bedtime resists being told and accepts being known. When the child can name and run the sequence — pillow, blanket, book, light — bedtime stops being something that happens to them.',
  know:'You need the card and the actual bedtime, which is the perfect classroom: the lesson IS the routine tonight, with each word said as each thing happens.',
  why:'The wardrobe-closet pair rides along free: whichever your family says, the child now owns both names for the same door. Bedside table is the bonus word — it belongs to the child.',
  dev:'Sequencing (first pillow, then blanket, then light), ownership of a routine, and the possessive pride of my bed, my pillow, my table.',
  vocab:'bed, pillow, blanket, wardrobe, closet, bedside table',
  teach:'1) Show the card at the bedroom door: "five words, and we will do every one of them tonight."\n2) Wardrobe first: open it, name the clothes hanging there — closet or wardrobe, say yours, offer both.\n3) Bed next: pillow fluffed by small hands, blanket spread by the same.\n4) Book on the bedside table — the table gets its word as the book lands.\n5) Light off with the fifth word: "bedroom words — all five. Goodnight."',
  say:'"Bedroom words, in order: clothes in the wardrobe, pillow up, blanket down, book on the bedside table, light off. You know the whole goodnight by heart."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Bedroom Words" sit over a made bed, a soft pillow, a folded blanket with a stripe, a tall wardrobe with two doors and a small bedside table with a drawer.',
  ask:'"Where do the clothes sleep?" (the wardrobe — the closet) "What holds your head?" "Where does the storybook live until morning?"',
  childSay:'Your child might recite the five in bed like a countdown, insist the teddy has its own bedside spot, or teach the sequence to the wardrobe itself.',
  real:'Every bed away from home — hotel, grandma&rsquo;s, holiday — still has a pillow, a blanket and a light. The sequence goes where the child goes, and strange bedrooms shrink to familiar words.',
  play:'Goodnight Countdown: the five words written (or drawn) on a card by the bed; the child taps each one as it happens tonight. Five taps and the card says goodnight first.',
  check:'Ask: "What goes on first, the pillow or the blanket?" (pillow) "Where do we keep clothes?" (the wardrobe or closet) "What is the little table by the bed?" (the bedside table)'},
 'bedroom-objects':{
  title:'The Bedroom Objects Lesson',one:'Hand your child the two switches that run the bedroom: the curtains and the lamp.',
  seoTitle:'Bedroom Objects Lesson for Kids — Lamp, Curtain, Rug, Alarm Clock (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach bedroom objects — lamp, curtain, rug and alarm clock — by handing over the real switches that control the room.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches four small objects by giving their power away: the child who opens the curtains and clicks the lamp owns the room&rsquo;s light, and the words come with the ownership.',
  what:'The bedroom objects do the room&rsquo;s quiet work: the lamp makes light, the curtains manage the morning, the rug warms bare feet, the alarm clock keeps the time.',
  mean:'Objects a child controls are objects a child names. The curtain is a word twice a day — opened at wake, closed at night — and twice a day is a lot for one word.',
  know:'You need the card and the two reachable switches: curtains a child can pull and a lamp a child can click. The rug and the clock are pointed at, not switched.',
  why:'The alarm clock doubles as the first clock lesson: the short hand is where we wake. Objects that do visible jobs teach their names almost by themselves.',
  dev:'Cause and effect (curtain open means morning light), time-awareness seed (the clock hands), and the responsibility glow of a real job done daily.',
  vocab:'lamp, curtain, rug, alarm clock, light, morning',
  teach:'1) Show the card at wake-up time: "four objects, two of them are yours."\n2) Hand over the curtains: the child opens them, and the morning arrives — "you opened the day."\n3) Point the rug: bare feet land on it, "warm, because of the rug."\n4) Introduce the alarm clock: which hand is short? Where does it point when we wake?\n5) At story time, the lamp is the child&rsquo;s to click — light for reading, made by them.',
  say:'"Bedroom objects! You open the curtains — hello, sun. You click the lamp — hello, story. The rug keeps your feet warm and the clock keeps the morning."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Bedroom Objects" sit over a bed with a blue blanket, a lamp with a cream shade, blue curtains drawn aside, a striped rug and an alarm clock with bells on top.',
  ask:'"What makes the little pool of light at story time?" (the lamp) "What opens the morning?" (the curtains) "What says good morning before we do?"',
  childSay:'Your child might take curtain duty so seriously it becomes the household clock, set the alarm with a grown-up for practice, or click the lamp like a lighthouse keeper.',
  real:'Lamps and curtains run every room in every home — the living room lamp, the kitchen curtains. The child who runs the bedroom switches starts running them everywhere.',
  play:'Light and Morning: a two-word game — the child performs "curtain" (open wide, arms out) and "lamp" (one slow click, one blink) with their whole body. Call them fast, call them slow; the words get exercised either way.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we open at morning?" (the curtains) "What do we click at story time?" (the lamp) "What is warm under bare feet?" (the rug)'},
 'storage-words':{
  title:'The Storage Lesson',one:'Teach where things live — and tidy-up becomes a game of sending things home.',
  seoTitle:'Storage Lesson for Kids — Drawer, Cupboard, Shelf, Basket, Box (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach storage words — drawer, cupboard, shelf, basket and box — so that every toy and spoon has a home the child can name.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches storage as a home-for-everything: five words, five real homes in your house, and a game that turns tidy-up time into sending things back where they live.',
  what:'Storage is where things live when they are not being used: socks in the drawer, dishes in the cupboard, books on the shelf, toys in the basket, surprises in the box.',
  mean:'Tidy-up time fails when putting away has no address. Once each thing has a named home, the child is not cleaning — the child is rehoming, which is a completely different feeling.',
  know:'You need the card and five real storage homes your own house uses. Match the card where you can; the box can be any box — it is the most flexible member.',
  why:'The word cupboard wears its passport openly: UK homes say cupboard, US homes say cabinet or closet. Say yours, offer the other, and the storage map works in both countries.',
  dev:'Organization logic (a place for everything), matching (thing to home), and the tidy-up identity — I am the person who sends things home.',
  vocab:'drawer, cupboard, cabinet, shelf, basket, box, storage',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five homes — let us meet the real ones in our house."\n2) Open the drawer together: one thing in, one thing out, say its address — "the drawer."\n3) Cupboard or cabinet: the dishes live here; open, look, close gently.\n4) Shelf and basket: one book home, one toy home, said out loud.\n5) The box last — "the box is the most generous home, it takes anything" — and tidy-up begins.',
  say:'"Storage words! Everything in this house lives somewhere. The spoon lives in the drawer, the teddy lives in the basket — let us send everybody home."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Storage Words" sit over an open drawer, a two-door cupboard, a three-tier shelf, a woven basket with handles and a lidded box.',
  ask:'"Where does the spoon live?" "Where does the teddy live?" "Which storage home takes anything at all?" (the box)',
  childSay:'Your child might give every object an address all day — "sock, drawer!" — invent homes for things that lack them, or close every drawer in the house like a little landlord.',
  real:'Storage runs the whole world: the shop has shelves, the library has shelves, the car has a glove box. A child who knows storage words reads rooms everywhere.',
  play:'Send It Home: five things on the rug, five homes named. The child carries each one to its address — the last delivery gets a cheer. Pack away time is now the game, not the argument.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do the dishes live?" (the cupboard — the cabinet) "Where do the toys live?" (the basket) "What is the word for where things live?" (storage)'},
 'household-objects':{
  title:'The Household Objects Lesson',one:'Name the five quiet things that make a house feel like yours.',
  seoTitle:'Household Objects Lesson for Kids — Clock, Frame, Vase, Cushion, Curtain (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach household objects — clock, picture frame, vase, cushion and curtain — as the personal touches that make a house a home.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the soft furniture of belonging: the clock, the picture frame, the vase, the cushion and the curtain — the things nobody needs but every home keeps.',
  what:'Household objects are the small keepers of a home&rsquo;s feeling: a clock to tick, a frame to hold a face, a vase to hold flowers, a cushion to hold a head, a curtain to hold the day out.',
  mean:'These words carry feelings with them — the frame holds someone loved, the vase holds something grown. Vocabulary tied to affection is the vocabulary children keep.',
  know:'You need the card and a slow walk through your own home, visiting the real versions. Each one gets its word and one sentence about what it holds or does.',
  why:'This is the lesson where the child learns that homes are decorated on purpose — someone chose the frame, someone picked the flowers. Next comes "can we pick the flowers?"',
  dev:'Affective vocabulary (holds, keeps, loves), noticing detail (what is IN the frame?), and the cushion-curtain pair that appears in every soft room.',
  vocab:'clock, picture frame, vase, cushion, curtain, household',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five things that make our house ours — let us visit them."\n2) The clock: hold a finger to your ear-watching, tick, tick — what is it saying?\n3) The frame: who is in it? Say the name — the frame holds people we love.\n4) The vase: are there flowers today? Whose room has one?\n5) Cushion and curtain to finish: one to lean on, one to close the day — both words said twice.',
  say:'"Household objects — the keepers! The clock keeps the time, the frame keeps a face, the vase keeps the flowers, the cushion keeps your head, the curtain keeps the night."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Household Objects" sit over a round clock, a standing picture frame, a blue-striped vase, a soft square cushion and blue curtains with tied-back ties.',
  ask:'"What does the clock do all day?" (tick — keep the time) "Who is inside our picture frame?" "What holds flowers?"',
  childSay:'Your child might appoint the clock the house&rsquo;s heartbeat, choose which picture earns the frame spot next, or arrange every cushion into a throne before sitting.',
  real:'Frames and vases appear at every grandparent&rsquo;s house — and now the child can greet them by name and ask whose face that is. The words make visiting richer.',
  play:'Cushion Corner: build a reading corner with every cushion in the house, sit inside it, and name the household objects you can see from there. The softest vocabulary lesson available.',
  check:'Ask: "What keeps the time?" (the clock) "What holds someone we love?" (the picture frame) "What closes the day at bedtime?" (the curtain)'},
 'daily-routines':{
  title:'The Daily Routines Lesson',one:'Give the day its five-part shape — and hand your child the running of it.',
  seoTitle:'Daily Routines Lesson for Kids — The Five Steps of a Whole Day (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach the daily routine — wake up, get dressed, eat breakfast, brush teeth, go to bed — with a clothespin chart your child runs.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the day as a five-step story the child already stars in: the chart is made together, and from then on the clothespin does the arguing.',
  what:'A daily routine is the order a day keeps: wake up, get dressed, eat breakfast, brush teeth, and at the far end, go to bed — the same order, every day.',
  mean:'Routine is time made visible. A child who cannot read a clock can still read a clothespin on a chart, and the clothespin answers the only question that matters: what comes next?',
  know:'You need the card, a strip of paper, five quick drawings (or the card itself) and one clothespin. The chart goes at child height — its power is its reachability.',
  why:'Transitions are where the day fights back. The chart moves the argument from person to object: not "get dressed because I said" but "the pin moved — it is dressing time."',
  dev:'Sequencing and time concepts, self-management (the child moves the pin), and the deep comfort of predictability — the day has a shape and the shape is friendly.',
  vocab:'wake up, get dressed, breakfast, brush teeth, go to bed, routine',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five steps make one day — here they are in order."\n2) Read each step with its gesture: stretch (wake up), arms up (get dressed), spoon (breakfast), big grin (brush teeth), hands together (go to bed).\n3) Make the chart: five pictures on a strip, the child decorates, the clothespin sits at step one.\n4) All day, the child moves the pin at each step — announce it proudly.\n5) At bedtime the pin rests on go-to-bed: "the pin finished the day. So did you."',
  say:'"Daily routines — the day has five doors! Wake up, dressed, breakfast, teeth, bed. You are the pin-mover: when the pin moves, the day moves."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Daily Routines" sit over five small scenes in order: a bed with the sun waking, folded clothes, a breakfast bowl with milk, a toothbrush and paste, and a bedtime bed with a moon in the window.',
  ask:'"What comes after breakfast?" (brush teeth) "What is the last step of the day?" (go to bed) "Who moves the pin?" (you do!)',
  childSay:'Your child might move the pin before the step is done (and defend it fiercely), carry the chart around like a schedule, or ask where the pin goes at nap time.',
  real:'The five steps follow the child everywhere — holidays, weekends, grandma&rsquo;s house. The pin can travel in a pocket; the routine comes with it.',
  play:'Routine Charades: act one step silently — the child guesses and then performs it. Brushing teeth pantomime is the family favorite. Five acts, five guesses, one whole day practiced.',
  check:'Ask: "How many steps in our day?" (five) "What do we do after getting dressed?" (eat breakfast) "Who runs the chart?" (the child — the pin-mover)'},
 'bedroom-routines':{
  title:'The Bedroom Routines Lesson',one:'Make the bed the bookends of the day: one morning job, one night job.',
  seoTitle:'Bedroom Routines Lesson for Kids — Make the Bed, Open the Curtains, Lights Off (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach bedroom routines — making the bed, opening the curtains, getting dressed, the bedtime book and the light — morning to night in one room.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bedroom&rsquo;s day: five routines that happen in one room from morning to night, each one owned by the child who does it.',
  what:'The bedroom routines run twice daily: make the bed and open the curtains in the morning; get dressed, read the book and turn off the light at night.',
  mean:'A made bed is the day&rsquo;s first finished task and the room&rsquo;s nightly welcome. Children who close their own day — book, light — sleep on a story that ended well.',
  know:'You need the card, the bed and the curtains. The morning half takes three minutes; the night half is bedtime anyway — this lesson borrows it and names it.',
  why:'Morning jobs done in the bedroom mean the room is already won before breakfast. And the night job is the lesson&rsquo;s secret: turning off the light is the child saying the day is complete.',
  dev:'Task completion (pillows first, blanket up, done), daily responsibility, and the closing ritual that tells the body the day is over.',
  vocab:'make the bed, curtains, get dressed, read, turn off the light',
  teach:'1) Show the card in the morning: "the bedroom has jobs — here are all five."\n2) Make the bed together: pillows first, blanket up, corners as best they land. Announce it finished.\n3) Open the curtains: hello, day — the room is awake.\n4) At night, get dressed for bed, then the book: one story on the made bed.\n5) The child turns off the light — the fifth word, the last move, the room sleeps.',
  say:'"Bedroom routines! Morning: bed made, curtains open — the room is ready. Night: book read, light off — the room is sleeping. You ran the whole day in one room."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Bedroom Routines" sit over a bed being made, blue curtains opening on a window, a folded shirt and trousers, an open book and a bedside lamp with a hand reaching for the switch.',
  ask:'"What do we do first in the bedroom?" (make the bed) "What opens the morning?" (the curtains) "What is the very last thing at night?" (turn off the light)',
  childSay:'Your child might make the bed with military pride and forbid all sitting on it, read the book aloud to the blanket, or turn the light off with a ceremonial flourish.',
  real:'The made bed follows the child to every sleepover and hotel — the job travels. So does the closing ritual: book, light, goodnight, wherever the pillow is.',
  play:'Room Flip: time the morning half — bed and curtains, together, from lights-on to done. Beat yesterday&rsquo;s time by a smile, not a stopwatch. The room flips itself eventually.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we make in the morning?" (the bed) "What do we open?" (the curtains) "What closes the day?" (the light going off)'},
 'bathroom-words':{
  title:'The Bathroom Words Lesson',one:'Five big friends in one small room — named at the exact moment each is used.',
  seoTitle:'Bathroom Words Lesson for Kids — Sink, Bath, Shower, Towel, Soap (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach bathroom words — sink, bath, shower, towel and soap — live at bath time, with the tap-faucet pair said in one breath.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bathroom at bath time, when every word on the card is working at full volume: water running, soap bubbling, towel waiting.',
  what:'The bathroom keeps the sink, the bath, the shower, the towel and the soap — the five big friends that every wash day needs.',
  mean:'Bath time is the richest vocabulary hour of the day — warm, wet, daily and already loved. Words learned in the bath are learned for good.',
  know:'You need the card and a real bath or shower. Each word is said at the moment its thing happens — soap is taught as soap is used.',
  why:'The tap-faucet pair rides in naturally: the card says the water words, the family says whichever, and the child hears both without a lesson about countries.',
  dev:'Object vocabulary in real time, sensory anchoring (soap = slippery, towel = warm), and the sequencing of a wash — water, soap, rinse, dry.',
  vocab:'sink, bath, shower, towel, soap, tap, faucet, water',
  teach:'1) Show the card as the bath fills: "five friends are here tonight — let us say hello to each."\n2) Sink first, hands in: the sink word with the tap (faucet) running.\n3) Soap: one pump or one rub, bubbles up — the word sticks to the slipperiness.\n4) Bath or shower — whichever tonight: name it as the water pours.\n5) Towel last, warm from the rack: wrap, and the five are all met.',
  say:'"Bathroom words, live! The tap — some say faucet — brings the water, the soap makes the bubbles, the towel gives the hug. Hello to all five friends."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Bathroom Words" sit over a pedestal sink with a tap, a claw-foot bath, a glass shower with the water running, a hanging towel and a dish of green soap.',
  ask:'"What makes the bubbles?" (the soap) "What brings the water?" (the tap — the faucet) "What hugs you dry?" (the towel)',
  childSay:'Your child might pour for the toys while naming the whole bathroom, ask whether the shower or the bath is bigger, or demand the soap word again at every hand wash.',
  real:'Hotel bathrooms, swimming pools, grandparent houses — the five friends are always there. The child who met them at home greets them everywhere.',
  play:'Bath Band: each wash-day item gets a sound — tap goes drip, soap goes squidge, shower goes pssh, towel goes flap. The child plays the band while getting clean; the words rehearse themselves.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does the water come from?" (the tap — the faucet) "What do we scrub with?" (the soap) "What dries us?" (the towel)'},
 'more-bathroom-words':{
  title:'The More Bathroom Words Lesson',one:'Finish the room: the mirror, the mat and the toilet words, said plainly.',
  seoTitle:'More Bathroom Words Lesson for Kids — Mirror, Bath Mat, Toilet (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach the rest of the bathroom — bath mat, mirror, toothbrush holder, toilet and toilet paper — calmly, plainly and without giggles.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bathroom&rsquo;s remaining words — including the toilet ones — plainly and calmly, which is exactly how they stay useful.',
  what:'The rest of the bathroom: the bath mat under wet feet, the mirror above the sink, the toothbrush holder, the toilet and its paper.',
  mean:'Toilet words said plainly stay plainly useful for life. A child who can say what they need without embarrassment asks for help sooner, in every bathroom in the world.',
  know:'You need the card and the real room. The mirror does the heavy lifting — it turns the lesson into a show — and the mat gets its word underfoot.',
  why:'The holder is the surprise word: the child&rsquo;s own toothbrush lives in a named place. And the mat carries the safety half — wet feet slip, mats catch them.',
  dev:'Body vocabulary said calmly, self-care independence, and mirror work — faces, teeth, hair — that rehearses every grooming skill there is.',
  vocab:'bath mat, mirror, toothbrush holder, toilet, toilet paper',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "the bathroom has more words — here are the last five."\n2) Step on the mat: how does it feel? That is its job — catching wet feet.\n3) Mirror time: show your teeth, make a happy face, a surprised face — the mirror shows everything back.\n4) Open the holder: whose brush lives here? Name the holder by name.\n5) Toilet words last, plainly: the toilet and the toilet paper — everybody uses them, every day.',
  say:'"More bathroom words! The mat catches your feet, the mirror shows your grin, the holder keeps your brush — and the toilet words we say the same way we say all the others."',
  notice:'On this card the words "More Bathroom Words" sit over a bath mat, a rounded mirror, a cup holding two toothbrushes, a toilet with its lid down and a roll of toilet paper on its holder.',
  ask:'"What keeps wet feet from slipping?" (the bath mat) "What shows you your face?" (the mirror) "Where does your toothbrush live?" (the holder)',
  childSay:'Your child might check the mirror after every face-wash, straighten the mat like a ringmaster, or teach the toilet words to a teddy in exactly your calm voice.',
  real:'Every bathroom away from home has the same five — the mat, the mirror, the holder, the toilet, the paper. New bathrooms stop being unknown when the words already fit.',
  play:'Mirror Show: two minutes of mirror games — happy, surprised, sleepy, silly — ending with the biggest tooth-brushing grin. The mirror rehearses faces; the words rehearse along.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we step on with wet feet?" (the mat) "What do we see ourselves in?" (the mirror) "Where does the toothbrush stand?" (the holder)'},
 'bathroom-routines':{
  title:'The Bathroom Routines Lesson',one:'Turn five wash-day actions into songs the child starts without being asked.',
  seoTitle:'Bathroom Routines Lesson for Kids — Wash Hands, Brush Teeth, Wash Face (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach bathroom routines — hand washing, teeth, face, bath and drying — each with its own two-line song that does the remembering.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the bathroom as five actions with five songs: the singing does the sequencing, and the child runs the whole routine on melody alone.',
  what:'The bathroom routines are the verbs of the room: wash hands, brush teeth, wash face, take a bath, dry hands — done daily, in some order, forever.',
  mean:'Routines live on rails, and songs are rails. A two-line tune with the words inside — bubbles, bubbles, scrub and rinse — will outlast any reminder a grown-up can give.',
  know:'You need the card and any sink. Invent the two-line songs together on the spot; familiar tunes (happy birthday speed) work better than clever ones.',
  why:'Hand washing needs its twenty seconds — a song is the timer. Teeth need their two minutes — another song, longer. The music is not decoration; it is the mechanism.',
  dev:'Self-care sequences, timing by song (an early math sense), and the ownership of hygiene — the child who starts the song starts the routine.',
  vocab:'wash hands, brush teeth, wash face, take a bath, dry hands',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five jobs, five songs — let us write them."\n2) Hands song first, at the real sink: soap, bubbles, scrub, rinse — sing it twice through (that is the twenty seconds).\n3) Teeth song: up and down, front and back — long version, two minutes for the real brush.\n4) Face song at the wash: splish, tickle, done.\n5) Drying song to finish: towel, towel, dry dry dry — the forgotten half, now sung.',
  say:'"Bathroom routines — with the songs! Sing the hands song while the bubbles work, sing the teeth song while the brush runs. The music does the remembering."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Bathroom Routines" sit over five small scenes: hands under a running tap, a brush and toothpaste in a cup, hands splashing a face, a bubble-topped bath and hands drying on a towel rail.',
  ask:'"What song goes with the soap?" "How long do we brush?" (the whole teeth song) "What comes after washing — always?" (drying)',
  childSay:'Your child might sing the hands song at every random sink, conduct the teeth song with the brush, or assign the drying song to every towel in the house.',
  real:'The songs travel: any sink, any campsite, any grandparent&rsquo;s bathroom — the child starts singing and the washing happens on beat. Restrooms everywhere run on this.',
  play:'Song Swap: tonight the child composes a new line for one routine song and teaches it to you. Composer-led hygiene: the routine gets renewed by its owner.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we sing while the soap bubbles?" (the hands song) "What is the forgotten half of washing?" (drying) "Who starts the songs?" (you do)'},
 'washing-and-bathing':{
  title:'The Washing and Bathing Lesson',one:'Make bath night an equipped adventure — and the toys the training crew.',
  seoTitle:'Washing and Bathing Lesson for Kids — Shower Gel, Sponge, Bath Toys (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach washing and bathing words — shower gel, sponge, bath towel, bath toys and hair dryer — with a toy wash-day that rehearses the real one.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches bath night item by item, with the toys as the rehearsal crew: what the child does to the teddy, the child accepts for themselves.',
  what:'Bath night uses its own kit: the shower gel for bubbles, the sponge for scrubbing, the bath towel for wrapping, the bath toys for morale and the hair dryer for warm endings.',
  mean:'The toys are the mechanism: a child who scrubs a teddy&rsquo;s back with the sponge has rehearsed sitting still for their own. Every wash-day item gets a rehearsal.',
  know:'You need the card and the real kit at the real bath. The hair dryer is demonstrated, not handed over: warm air, grown-up hands, the cool button as the child&rsquo;s one press.',
  why:'Equipping the bath turns waiting into doing — the child manages the toys&rsquo; wash while their own hair gets washed. The kit words carry the night.',
  dev:'Sequenced self-care, pretend-play as rehearsal for real care, and the body-map that comes from naming where the sponge goes — arms, tummy, back.',
  vocab:'shower gel, sponge, bath towel, bath toys, hair dryer',
  teach:'1) Show the card as the water runs: "bath night has its own team — here they are."\n2) Shower gel: one squirt into the water, bubbles up — the word rides the smell.\n3) Sponge: the child scrubs the teddy first, then their own arms — arms, tummy, back, named.\n4) Toys washed and dried on the edge — the whole crew attends.\n5) Towel wrap at the end, and the hair dryer demo: warm air, one cool-button press, done.',
  say:'"Washing and bathing! Squirt the gel, scrub with the sponge — teddy first, then you. The towel hugs you out and the dryer hums you warm."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Washing and Bathing" sit over a bottle of shower gel, a blue sponge, a hanging bath towel with a stripe, a rubber duck beside a toy boat and a hair dryer.',
  ask:'"What makes the bubbles in the water?" (the shower gel) "What do we scrub with?" (the sponge) "What wraps you after?" (the bath towel)',
  childSay:'Your child might run a full wash-day for every toy, request the sponge by name each night, or press the cool button for every stuffed animal in the house.',
  real:'Swimming lessons, beach days and hotel baths all run on this kit — gel, towel, dry. A child who knows the words brings their own routine to any tub.',
  play:'Toy Wash Day: teddy and duck get the full service — gel, sponge, rinse, towel — narrated by the child. Then the child takes the same service. The order never changes: toys first.',
  safety:'The hair dryer is a grown-up tool, always: warm air only, never the hot setting near skin, cord clear of the water. The child presses the cool button; the grown-up holds the machine.'},
 'personal-care':{
  title:'The Personal Care Lesson',one:'Own your own kit: the brush, the comb and the tools nobody borrows.',
  seoTitle:'Personal Care Lesson for Kids — Toothbrush, Toothpaste, Hairbrush, Comb (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach personal care — toothbrush, toothpaste, hairbrush, comb and tissue — as a kit the child owns, uses and never shares.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches personal care as ownership: the child&rsquo;s own kit, the child&rsquo;s own routine, and the one rule — personal means personal.',
  what:'Personal care is the care of your own body with your own tools: the toothbrush and toothpaste, the hairbrush and the comb, the tissue for any nose.',
  mean:'Mine is the magic word of this lesson. A named, owned kit is a used kit — and the nobody-borrows rule teaches hygiene and boundaries in the same breath.',
  know:'You need the card and the real kit: brush, paste, hairbrush or comb (both if you have them), tissues. The kit gets a home of its own — that is the lesson&rsquo;s anchor.',
  why:'The brush-comb pair is a gentle first comparison: teeth different, hair different — two tools, two jobs. And the tissue completes the set for every nose in every season.',
  dev:'Self-care independence, hygiene rules (no sharing of personal tools), and sequence-ownership: the child runs their own two-minute routine.',
  vocab:'toothbrush, toothpaste, hairbrush, comb, tissue, personal',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "this is a personal kit — yours, nobody else&rsquo;s."\n2) Name each tool at its home: the brush and paste in the holder, the hairbrush and comb, the tissue box.\n3) Run the routine with the child leading: paste on, brush round, hair brushed or combed.\n4) Say the rule warmly: personal tools are not borrowed — nobody uses your brush, you use nobody&rsquo;s.\n5) Tissue last: one blow, into the bin — the kit is complete.',
  say:'"Personal care — your kit, your routine! Brush and paste for the teeth, brush and comb for the hair, tissue for the nose. And the rule: personal tools are personal."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Personal Care" sit over a blue-handled toothbrush, a tube of toothpaste, a paddle hairbrush, a wide-tooth comb and a blue tissue box with one tissue peeking out.',
  ask:'"Which tools are yours and nobody else&rsquo;s?" "What does the comb do that the brush does too?" "Where does a used tissue go?" (in the bin)',
  childSay:'Your child might guard the kit like a dragon, comb every doll&rsquo;s hair with their own comb, or recite the nobody-borrows rule unprompted at the dentist.',
  real:'Sleepovers and hotel stays test the kit: a child with their own travel brush and comb keeps the routine anywhere. The rule follows the tools.',
  play:'Kit Check: at the morning routine, the child names their tools as they use them — brush, paste, brush, comb — and closes with the rule. Full kit named is a full kit used.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we put on the brush?" (the toothpaste) "What has wide teeth for tangles?" (the comb) "Who may use your toothbrush?" (nobody — it is personal)'},
 'kitchen-actions':{
  title:'The Kitchen Actions Lesson',one:'Teach the kitchen as verbs — and give the child one real verb per evening.',
  seoTitle:'Kitchen Actions Lesson for Kids — Cook, Set the Table, Wash, Pour (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach kitchen actions — cook dinner, set the table, wash dishes, open the fridge, pour a drink — with one real job per evening.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the kitchen through its verbs: the room is not things but doings, and the child gets one doing to own each evening.',
  what:'The kitchen runs on actions: cook dinner, set the table, wash the dishes, open the fridge, pour a drink — the five verbs this card puts on display.',
  mean:'Action words are the grammar of helping. A child who owns set-the-table has a role, not an assignment — and roles are what make a family engine run.',
  know:'You need the card and any meal. The verbs are taught live: set the table as the table is set, pour as the water pours (small jug, big glass, steady hands).',
  why:'One real verb per evening beats all the pretend cooking in the world. The child who sets the table tonight reads the verb card differently tomorrow.',
  dev:'Verb vocabulary, task persistence (a set table is a finished task), and the pour — a fine-motor classic that doubles as a patience lesson.',
  vocab:'cook, set the table, wash dishes, fridge, pour, kitchen',
  teach:'1) Show the card before dinner: "the kitchen is a room of verbs — you get one tonight."\n2) Read the five actions with their mime: stir, lay, scrub, open, pour.\n3) Assign tonight&rsquo;s verb — setting the table is the best starter: plate, cup, spoon, named as they land.\n4) Open the fridge together: what does the fridge keep? Cold things — name two.\n5) Pour last: the child pours their own water from a small jug. Spills are data, not disasters.',
  say:'"Kitchen actions! Cook, set, wash, open, pour — pick your verb for tonight. The table is waiting for its plate, cup and spoon."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Kitchen Actions" sit over five small scenes: a steaming pot on the stove, a laid place setting, a sink of dishes with bubbles, an open fridge full of food and a jug pouring a glass.',
  ask:'"What do we do before dinner lands?" (set the table) "What keeps the food cold?" (the fridge) "Which verb did you do tonight?"',
  childSay:'Your child might assign verbs to the whole family like a head chef, pour everything in sight (water, oats, bath toys), or open the fridge just to greet the cold.',
  real:'Restaurants, cafés and grandma&rsquo;s kitchen all run on the same five verbs. The child who sets tables at home sets them anywhere — and notices when nobody has.',
  play:'Verb Dice: five verbs on five paper squares, pick one blind each evening — that is tonight&rsquo;s job. The draw is the ceremony; the doing is the lesson; the fridge-opening is always popular.',
  safety:'The stove and the oven are grown-up verbs — the child stirs only from a stool, with a grown-up beside and the pan handle turned inward. Say it with the cook verb, every time.'},
 'home-appliances':{
  title:'The Home Appliances Lesson',one:'Meet the helpful machines — and learn each one&rsquo;s sound and its rules.',
  seoTitle:'Home Appliances Lesson for Kids — Television, Toaster, Fan, Iron (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach home appliances — television, fan, computer, toaster and iron — by sound first, with the toaster as the safe, delicious demonstration.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches appliances by their sounds: whirr, pop, beep — the house is full of machine voices, and each one belongs to a named machine with its own rules.',
  what:'Home appliances are the machines that help: the television shows, the fan cools, the computer works, the toaster toasts, the iron presses.',
  mean:'Machines with rules are the first machines: the toaster is everyone&rsquo;s (with a grown-up), the iron is nobody&rsquo;s (but a grown-up). Machine etiquette starts here and scales to everything with a plug.',
  know:'You need the card and a sound hunt: which machines can you hear right now? The toaster demonstration is the finale — bread in, count, pop, toast.',
  why:'Sound-first naming works because machines announce themselves: the child learns which whirr is the fan and which pop is the toaster, and the house becomes legible by ear.',
  dev:'Sound-object matching, machine safety categories (mine with help / grown-up only), and the anticipation-timing of the toaster countdown.',
  vocab:'television, fan, computer, toaster, iron, machine',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five machines help this house — let us hear them."\n2) Sound hunt first: sit still, which machines are talking? Fan? Fridge hum? Name what you find.\n3) Match the sounds to the card: whirr goes the fan, pop goes the toaster.\n4) Toaster demonstration with a grown-up: bread in, count together, pop — toast earned.\n5) The rules said kindly: the iron and the stove are grown-up machines; we look, we learn, we do not touch.',
  say:'"Home appliances! The fan goes whirr, the toaster goes pop, the television says the story. Which machine is talking right now?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Home Appliances" sit over a wide television on its stand, an electric fan, a desktop computer with keyboard and mouse, a toaster with two slices and a blue iron.',
  ask:'"Which machine pops?" (the toaster) "Which machine cools us on hot days?" (the fan) "Which machines are grown-up only?" (the iron — and the stove)',
  childSay:'Your child might announce every machine sound in the house like a sports commentator, count to pop with real faith, or ask what the computer is always working on.',
  real:'Every house hums with the same machines — hotels, holidays, grandparent visits. The child who knows the sounds can map any new house in one quiet minute.',
  play:'Machine Orchestra: the family assigns each heard machine a gesture — whirr (spin a finger), pop (jump), beep (tap a nose) — and plays the house as a symphony. The appliances teach themselves while you laugh.',
  safety:'Plugs and cords are grown-up territory, said with the words: machines get their power from the wall, and the wall side is never a child&rsquo;s job. The toaster happens beside a grown-up, always.'},
 'shopping-words':{
  title:'The Shopping Words Lesson',one:'Give the shop trip five words and one picture list — and your child becomes the finder.',
  seoTitle:'Shopping Words Lesson for Kids — Bag, Basket, Wallet, Trolley, Cart (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach shopping words — shopping bag, basket, receipt, wallet, trolley — with a picture list that turns the weekly shop into a finding game.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the shop before you get there: five words for the equipment of shopping, and a picture list that gives the child a job worth racing for.',
  what:'Shopping runs on its equipment: the shopping bag that carries, the basket for small trips, the trolley (the cart) for big ones, the wallet that pays, the receipt that proves.',
  mean:'A child with a job at the shop is a partner, not cargo. The finder role — apples, bread, soap — converts aisle whining into aisle hunting, every single time.',
  know:'You need the card, paper and a crayon for the picture list, and one real shop trip. The trolley-cart pair is said openly: same machine, two countries, two names.',
  why:'The wallet-receipt pair teaches paying in two beats: money from the wallet, proof from the receipt. Abstract money grows a paper trail a child can hold.',
  dev:'List-reading (pictures before words), category hunting in a real environment, and the money script — pay, receipt, bag — rehearsed honestly.',
  vocab:'shopping bag, basket, receipt, wallet, trolley, shopping cart',
  teach:'1) Show the card before the trip: "the shop has equipment — learn it and you run it."\n2) Name the carry-crew: bag, basket, trolley — or cart, say both, pick your favorite out loud.\n3) Make the picture list together: three to five things, drawn by the child.\n4) At the shop: basket first, then the hunt — the child finds and crosses off.\n5) Wallet and receipt at the till: watch the pay, take the receipt, bag it up — the whole card, lived.',
  say:'"Shopping words! Bag, basket, trolley — cart, if you like that word better. Wallet pays, receipt proves, and you — you are the finder today."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Shopping Words" sit over a blue shopping bag with a house mark, a woven basket, a paper receipt, a blue wallet and a wire trolley.',
  ask:'"What do we push at the big shop?" (the trolley — the cart) "What pays?" (the wallet) "What proves we paid?" (the receipt)',
  childSay:'Your child might cross off list items with a flourish, choose cart or trolley as a personal identity, or demand the receipt like a tiny accountant.',
  real:'Every shop in every country runs this equipment — the words convert at the border (trolley to cart, receipt stays receipt). A child with a list shops happily in any language.',
  play:'Shop at Home: line up pantry goods, hand over the picture list, a basket and a toy wallet — the child shops the hallway. Real receipts optional; the pride is not.',
  check:'Ask: "What carries the shopping home?" (the shopping bag) "What is the big wheeled one called?" (trolley — or cart) "Who was the finder today?" (the child)'},
 'cleaning-and-tidying':{
  title:'The Cleaning and Tidying Lesson',one:'Equip a small cleaner with real tools — the water-spray bottle does the recruiting.',
  seoTitle:'Cleaning and Tidying Lesson for Kids — Sponge, Cloth, Bin, Vacuum (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach cleaning and tidying — sponge, cloth, spray bottle, bin and vacuum cleaner — with a water-only spray kit and a one-job rule.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches cleaning as a trade with tools: five words, each tied to a real job a child can own — and the vacuum cleaner is the crown jewel.',
  what:'The cleaning kit has five members: the sponge for spills, the cloth for wiping, the spray bottle for wetting the wipe, the bin for the rubbish, the vacuum cleaner for the floors.',
  mean:'Children do not want chores; they want tools. Give a child their own spray bottle (water only) and a cloth, and the table-wiping job applies for itself daily.',
  know:'You need the card, a small cloth, a sponge and one empty spray bottle filled with water — labeled with the child&rsquo;s name. The bin and the vacuum get the field trips.',
  why:'Bin, trash can, garbage can — this lesson says all three once, then picks the family&rsquo;s. The word matters less than the rule that rides with it: in the bin and it stays.',
  dev:'Tool-use skills (spray, wipe, scoop), sequence thinking (spray, then wipe, then bin), and work pride — the family floor looks better because of you.',
  vocab:'sponge, cloth, spray bottle, bin, trash can, vacuum cleaner',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "cleaners have tools — today you get your own."\n2) Fill the spray bottle together: water only, name labeled, trigger practiced on the sink first.\n3) The table job: spray once, wipe with the cloth, fold and hang the cloth — the full professional sequence.\n4) Sponge to the bathroom sink: one spill, one press, wring it out — squeeze is the fun part.\n5) Vacuum finale: the child presses the switch, steers a straight line beside you, and the word VROOOM belongs to them.',
  say:'"Cleaning and tidying — the tool kit! Spray, wipe, bin, and the big VRRROOM machine. You have your own bottle now; the table has been asking for you."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Cleaning and Tidying" sit over a two-tone sponge, a folded blue cloth, a spray bottle with a trigger, a pedal bin and a canister vacuum cleaner with its hose.',
  ask:'"What do we spray on the table?" (water — only water) "What catches the crumbs?" "Which machine eats the dust?" (the vacuum cleaner)',
  childSay:'Your child might spray everything except the table, demand the vacuum for every crumb in the postcode, or narrate the bin like a hungry monster being fed.',
  real:'Spills happen everywhere — restaurants, grandma&rsquo;s, the car. A child with wipe skills becomes the designated helper, and helpers are never bored.',
  play:'Crumby Café: sprinkle cereal bits on a tray, and the child runs the whole cleanup — sweep to the dustpan, tip to the bin, wipe the tray. Restaurant closed, kitchen spotless, job very well done.',
  safety:'The spray bottle is WATER ONLY and lives apart from real cleaning products — say it plainly: grown-up cleaners are not for children, ever. The vacuum&rsquo;s cord stays behind the child, and the bin stays unexplored.'},
 'cleaning-tools':{
  title:'The Cleaning Tools Lesson',one:'One tool, one job — brooms sweep, mops wash, dusters tickle.',
  seoTitle:'Cleaning Tools Lesson for Kids — Broom, Mop, Bucket, Dustpan, Duster (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach cleaning tools — broom, mop, bucket, dustpan and duster — with the crumb-hunt game that makes floor care a child&rsquo;s department.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the big tools by their one jobs: each tool sweeps, washes or catches exactly one thing, and the child learns the whole assembly line.',
  what:'The floor-and-dust team: the broom sweeps, the mop washes, the bucket carries, the dustpan catches, the duster tickles the shelves.',
  mean:'Tools with one clear job teach systems: crumbs are swept, then caught, then binned — a three-step pipeline a child can run end to end with nothing but pride.',
  know:'You need the card and the real tools, ideally a child-size broom or at least a personal dustpan. The duster is the instant hit — hand it over first.',
  why:'The broom-mop distinction is the lesson&rsquo;s brain-stretch: both have long handles, but one is for dry crumbs and one is for wet floors. Same shape, different job — thinking begins.',
  dev:'Tool-function matching, pipeline sequencing (sweep, catch, tip), and the fine grip of duster work — plus the dignity of real equipment, not toys.',
  vocab:'broom, mop, bucket, dustpan, duster, sweep',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five tools, five jobs — meet the team."\n2) Duster first: the child dusts one shelf, tickling everything once — instant success.\n3) Broom and dustpan: sweep crumbs toward the dustpan, catch, and tip them into the bin — the pipeline, run once.\n4) Mop and bucket: show how the mop drinks from the bucket, then washes one floor square — the wet half of the family.\n5) End with the one-job chant: brooms sweep, mops wash, dusters tickle.',
  say:'"Cleaning tools, one job each! The broom sweeps — swish. The dustpan catches — plop. The duster tickles — choo! Which tool do you want today?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Cleaning Tools" sit over a straw broom, a string mop, a blue bucket, a blue dustpan with its handle and a feather duster.',
  ask:'"Which tool washes the floor?" (the mop) "Which one catches the crumbs?" (the dustpan) "What does the duster do?" (tickles the shelves clean)',
  childSay:'Your child might appoint themselves dust officer for life, sweep the same square for twenty happy minutes, or explain the one-job rule to visitors unprompted.',
  real:'Cafés, shops and school halls all have the same team standing in the corner. The child who knows the tools can identify the whole cleaning closet anywhere.',
  play:'Crumb Hunt: hide ten paper crumbs around one room; the child sweeps (hand broom or hand as broom), catches each in the dustpan and delivers them to the bin. Ten found, floor spotless, worker triumphant.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the broom do?" (sweeps) "What drinks from the bucket?" (the mop) "Where do the crumbs go after the dustpan?" (in the bin)'},
 'laundry-words':{
  title:'The Laundry Lesson',one:'Follow the clothes&rsquo; whole journey — machine, line, pegs, iron — and own the loading.',
  seoTitle:'Laundry Lesson for Kids — Washing Machine, Detergent, Clothesline (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach laundry words — washing machine, detergent, clothesline, clothespin and iron — with a real load, a mini line and the peg-washing-line pair.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches laundry as a journey the clothes take: dirty to bubbly to line to folded, with the child as the loader, the counter and the peg-clipping crew.',
  what:'Laundry words follow the wash: the washing machine tumbles, the detergent bubbles, the clothesline dries, the clothespins hold, the iron smooths.',
  mean:'The machine is mesmerizing — round and round — and the child who loads it with you owns a piece of the magic. Washing line and pegs ride alongside clothesline and clothespin: same journey, two shores.',
  know:'You need the card, one real load of washing, and (for the mini version) a string, two chairs and two pegs. The iron is demonstrated only, and from a safe distance.',
  why:'Sorting colors is a real sorting game with a consequence — a red sock incident is a legend that teaches faster than any lecture. The child sorts; the family remembers.',
  dev:'Sorting by category (colors), measuring by counting (one scoop — the grown-up&rsquo;s hand, the child&rsquo;s count), sequencing a multi-day process, and peg work for little fingers.',
  vocab:'washing machine, detergent, clothesline, washing line, clothespin, peg, iron',
  teach:'1) Show the card by the machine: "the clothes are going on a journey — here is the map."\n2) Sort with the child: colors with colors — darks here, lights there, and the story of why, told briefly.\n3) Load together: clothes in, detergent measured by the grown-up, counted by the child, door shut, start pressed.\n4) Watch the window a moment — round and round — then hang: line or rack, pegs (pins) clipped by small hands.\n5) The iron is the finale you only watch: warm plate, smooth moves, grown-up hands, wrinkle gone.',
  say:'"Laundry words! The machine tumbles round and round, the detergent makes the bubbles, the line holds the clothes up in the sun. Clip the pegs — you are the clothes&rsquo; anchor."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Laundry Words" sit over a front-loading washing machine mid-cycle, a bottle of detergent, a clothesline with a shirt and socks hanging, a wooden clothespin and a blue iron.',
  ask:'"Where do the clothes get clean?" (the washing machine) "What holds them on the line?" (the clothespin — the peg) "What makes the bubbles?" (the detergent)',
  childSay:'Your child might guard the machine window like drive-in cinema, peg everything in reach (including curtains), or ask when the iron gets to be theirs.',
  real:'Every home in every country runs this journey — hotels have laundry chutes, holidays have laundromats. The child who knows the words narrates the whole trip.',
  play:'Mini Line: string between two chairs, two socks, two pegs — the child hangs the washing, takes it down, folds it into a basket. The whole journey in miniature, run by the smallest crew member.',
  safety:'Detergent is a grown-up pour — pods especially are never play things, and the box lives up high. The iron is look-don&rsquo;t-touch until the far future: hot plate, grown-up hands, always.'},
 'outdoor-chores':{
  title:'The Outdoor Chores Lesson',one:'Five outside jobs that feel like play — because outside is where chores breathe.',
  seoTitle:'Outdoor Chores Lesson for Kids — Sweep, Water Plants, Feed Birds, Wash Car (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach outdoor chores — sweeping the path, watering plants, picking up leaves, feeding the birds and washing the car — with water as the reward.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the outside jobs: the path, the plants, the leaves, the birds and the car — chores that wear costumes of play and work just as hard.',
  what:'Outdoor chores keep the home&rsquo;s outside alive: sweep the path, water the plants, pick up the leaves, feed the birds, wash the car.',
  mean:'Water is the hinge of this whole lesson: watering cans, buckets, sponges and hoses are chore tools and toy boxes at once. The work gets done because the water is wonderful.',
  know:'You need the card, a small watering can, a bucket with a sponge, and a patch of path or leaves. The birds need only a feeder or a sprinkle of seeds and some quiet.',
  why:'Feeding the birds is the empathy chore: something alive depends on the showing up. And leaf-piles are the only chore with a built-in celebration clause — jump first, bag second.',
  dev:'Gross-motor work (sweeping, scrubbing circles), care for living things, and the outside-in rhythm of a job with a natural finish — the path is clean, the car is shiny.',
  vocab:'sweep the path, water the plants, pick up leaves, feed the birds, wash the car',
  teach:'1) Show the card outside: "five jobs — all of them better than inside jobs."\n2) Sweep the path together: long strokes, one direction, leaves gather like magic.\n3) Water the plants: glug, glug — one can, many pots, count as you go.\n4) Leaf pile: gather, jump (mandatory), then bag — celebration is part of the pipeline.\n5) Birds and car to close: seeds sprinkled quietly, then the bubbly circles on the car — the sponge and bucket finale.',
  say:'"Outdoor chores! Sweep the path, water the plants, pile the leaves, feed the birds — and the car gets its bath. Everything outside drinks or shines today."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Outdoor Chores" sit over five small scenes: a broom sweeping a path, a watering can over a potted plant, a rake and leaf pile, a birdhouse and bird by a feeder, and a soapy car mid-wash.',
  ask:'"Who do we feed in the morning?" (the birds) "What do the plants drink?" (water from the can) "Which chore lets you jump in the pile first?"',
  childSay:'Your child might water the same plant until it swims, choreograph a leaf-pile jump routine, or stand sentry at the bird feeder shushing the whole street.',
  real:'The same five jobs exist at every grandparent&rsquo;s and every holiday rental: paths, plants, birds, cars. A child with outdoor-chore skills is welcome everywhere.',
  play:'Water the Circuit: five cups of water, five thirsty things — a plant, the bird bath, the car&rsquo;s one panel, the path&rsquo;s one corner, the child&rsquo;s own hands. The circuit runs until the cups are empty and the job is genuinely done.',
  check:'Ask: "What do the plants drink from?" (the watering can) "What do the birds eat?" (the seeds) "What do we do before we bag the leaves?" (jump in the pile)'},
 'garden-tools':{
  title:'The Garden Tools Lesson',one:'Small hands, real tools: the trowel is the child&rsquo;s shovel, and the wheelbarrow is the ride.',
  seoTitle:'Garden Tools Lesson for Kids — Shovel, Rake, Trowel, Wheelbarrow (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach garden tools — shovel, rake, trowel, gloves and wheelbarrow — with a one-pot digging job sized for small hands.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the garden&rsquo;s team: five tools, each with a one-job rule, and the trowel handed to the child as their very own shovel.',
  what:'The garden runs on its tools: the shovel digs deep, the rake gathers, the trowel plants, the gloves guard the hands, the wheelbarrow hauls it all.',
  mean:'Real tools, honestly given, are how children join the work. A child with their own trowel is not watching gardening — the child is gardening.',
  know:'You need the card and the real tools. The digging job is one pot or one small patch: trowel, hole, seed or seedling, done. Gloves go on first, every time — that is the ritual.',
  why:'The yard-garden word rides along quietly: US families say yard, UK families say garden, the tools are identical. The one-job rule (shovels dig, rakes gather) is the lesson&rsquo;s spine.',
  dev:'Tool discipline (one job each), fine and gross motor work (trowel grip, rake pull), and the sequence of planting — dig, place, cover, water.',
  vocab:'shovel, rake, trowel, gardening gloves, wheelbarrow, garden, yard',
  teach:'1) Show the card outside: "the garden has a tool team — gloves first."\n2) Gloves on: the ritual that says work is beginning — wiggle each finger home.\n3) Meet the big tools by sight: the shovel digs deep holes, the rake gathers leaves — rrrike.\n4) The trowel is yours: one pot, one small hole, one seedling or seed in, soil patted down.\n5) Wheelbarrow finale: load the pots or leaves in, and the child takes one ride as the foreman.',
  say:'"Garden tools! Shovel digs, rake gathers, trowel plants — the trowel is YOUR little shovel. Gloves on, one hole to dig, and the wheelbarrow waits for its driver."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Garden Tools" sit over a blue shovel, a rake, a hand trowel, a pair of cream gardening gloves and a blue wheelbarrow with one wheel.',
  ask:'"Which tool digs the small holes?" (the trowel) "What do we put on first?" (the gloves) "Which tool carries everything?" (the wheelbarrow)',
  childSay:'Your child might sleep with the trowel, rake every leaf in the postcode into one noble pile, or ride the wheelbarrow and refuse to disembark.',
  real:'Community gardens, farms and grandma&rsquo;s yard all run the same team. The child who knows trowel from shovel is handed real jobs at every garden gate.',
  play:'Tool Race (gentle edition): three picture cards — shovel, rake, trowel — and three jobs called out. The child runs to touch the right real tool. Digging bonus: one real hole in one real pot, dug entirely by them.',
  safety:'Tools are for their jobs and only their jobs, and the pointy ends stay down and away. The wheelbarrow ride happens with a grown-up steering. Gloves are the ritual that starts every session.'},
 'tools-and-fix-it':{
  title:'The Tools and Fix-It Lesson',one:'Meet the repair heroes — and give hammering its one safe, supervised debut.',
  seoTitle:'Tools and Fix-It Lesson for Kids — Hammer, Screwdriver, Saw, Toolbox (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach tools and fix-it — hammer, screwdriver, saw and toolbox — with one real, goggled, fully supervised hammering session.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the fixing kit with its one rule riding alongside: tools are for fixing, real tools are used with a grown-up — and the hammer gets its honest debut.',
  what:'The fix-it team: the hammer drives nails, the screwdriver twists screws, the saw cuts (grown-ups only), and the toolbox is the home that latches them all in.',
  mean:'Curiosity about tools is not misbehavior — it is engineering interest with nowhere to go. One real hammering session, goggles on, is worth a year of don&rsquo;t-touch.',
  know:'You need the card, a small hammer, a block of scrap wood, two nails started by you, and child-size goggles if you have them. The saw and the screwdriver stay demonstrated.',
  why:'The toolbox latch is the lesson&rsquo;s quiet star: tools live in their home and come out for jobs. The click of the latch closing is the ritual ending of every fix-it session.',
  dev:'Hammer control (aim, swing, follow-through), respect for edged tools, and the fix-it identity — broken things are projects, not tragedies.',
  vocab:'hammer, screwdriver, saw, toolbox, nails, fix',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "the fix-it team sleeps in the toolbox — let us meet them."\n2) Open the toolbox together and name everyone: hammer, screwdriver, saw — each with its one job.\n3) Hammer debut: goggles on, nails started by the grown-up, the child drives them in — real wood, real sound, real pride.\n4) Screwdriver turn: one big screw in a soft block, twist, twist, twist.\n5) The saw is only waved at: back and forth motion in the air, and the rule said kindly — saws are grown-up tools, always.',
  say:'"Tools and fix-it! The hammer goes bang, the screwdriver goes twist, the saw is a grown-up machine. Today YOU are the fixer — goggles on, one nail at a time."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Tools and Fix-It" sit over a claw hammer with a pink grip, a red-handled screwdriver, a hand saw, and a latched two-tier toolbox.',
  ask:'"Which tool goes bang?" (the hammer) "Which tool twists?" (the screwdriver) "Where do the tools sleep?" (the toolbox — latched)',
  childSay:'Your child might carry the goggles everywhere for a week, fix toys that were never broken, or close the toolbox latch with the gravity of a master mechanic.',
  real:'The fix-it instinct lasts: a child who has hammered real nails builds, repairs and helps for years. Every parent of a teenager with a flat-pack wardrobe thanks this lesson.',
  play:'Fix-It Clinic: gather one squeaky toy, one loose screw, one wobbly thing (all pre-approved) — the child diagnoses, you assist, the hammer or screwdriver does its true job, the latch clicks shut. Clinic closed.',
  safety:'Real tools, one rule, said the same way every time: tools come out for fixing, with a grown-up, and go home to the toolbox after. The saw is demonstrated in the air and never nearer. Goggles first, hammer second.'},
 'more-tools':{
  title:'The More Tools Lesson',one:'Beyond the hammer: the drill, the pliers, the wrench — and the ruler that measures everything.',
  seoTitle:'More Tools Lesson for Kids — Drill, Pliers, Ruler, Wrench (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach more tools — drill, pliers, ruler, nails and wrench — with the ruler as the child&rsquo;s own first measuring instrument.',
  intro:'This lesson goes deeper into the toolbox for children who already know the basics: three grip-and-turn tools, one measuring friend, and the rule about pointy things.',
  what:'The deeper toolbox: the drill spins, the pliers grip, the wrench turns big bolts, the ruler measures, and the nails wait pointy side down.',
  mean:'The ruler is the gateway tool: it measures anything, hurts nobody, and quietly teaches numbers. Give a child a ruler and they will measure the house — that is not a bug.',
  know:'You need the card and a real ruler (paper or wooden). The drill is a demonstration at most — unplugged, trigger untouched — and the pliers and wrench are shown gripping, not wielded.',
  why:'Grip-think is the new idea here: pliers grip, wrenches turn, and both hold things so hands do not have to. Plus the safety rhyme of the set: pointy things point down.',
  dev:'Measurement sense (longer, shorter, how many), tool-function matching, and the grown-up-only categories said with warmth rather than worry.',
  vocab:'drill, pliers, ruler, nails, wrench, measure',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "more tools for bigger jobs — and one friend for measuring."\n2) The ruler first, into the child&rsquo;s hands: measure a shoe, a spoon, a book — numbers happen by themselves.\n3) Pliers demo: grip a soft sponge — squeeze, hold, release. That is their whole magic: gripping.\n4) Wrench: fit it over a big bolt (or a jar lid, safely still) — turn is the job.\n5) Drill: unplugged, shown spinning nothing — bzzzzt in the story, grown-up in the house. Nails last: pointy side down, always.',
  say:'"More tools! Pliers grip, the wrench turns, the drill spins for the grown-ups — and the ruler? The ruler measures EVERYTHING. Starting with your shoe."',
  notice:'On this card the words "More Tools" sit over a power drill, pliers with blue grips, a wooden ruler marked with numbers, a scatter of nails and a wide wrench.',
  ask:'"Which tool grips?" (the pliers) "Which tool measures?" (the ruler) "Which way do the nails point in the tin?" (pointy side down)',
  childSay:'Your child might measure the cat (with negotiations), grip everything with two fingers like pliers, or declare the drill the loudest tool in the book.',
  real:'Measuring is everywhere — shops, post offices, craft tables. The child with a ruler and the habit of asking how-long has a math tool that fits in a pencil case.',
  play:'Measure the House: the ruler and a notebook, five things to measure — shoe, book, spoon, teddy, doorway. The child measures, you write the numbers, the notebook becomes the house&rsquo;s first survey.',
  safety:'The drill is a grown-up machine, unplugged when shown. Nails point down and travel nowhere. And the tool-home rule holds: every tool returns to the toolbox, latch clicked.'},
 'by-the-door':{
  title:'The By the Door Lesson',one:'Build a launchpad: hook, basket, mat — and leaving the house becomes a checklist.',
  seoTitle:'By the Door Lesson for Kids — Key, Doormat, Umbrella, Hat, Shoe (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach by-the-door words — key, doormat, umbrella, hat and shoe — by building a real launchpad spot your child runs.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the door zone by building it: a hook, a basket and a mat, with every word on the card assigned to a real place your child owns.',
  what:'By the door lives the going-out kit: the key that unlocks, the doormat that wipes, the umbrella for rain, the hat for sun, the shoes pointing out.',
  mean:'Mornings collapse in the doorway. A launchpad with named places — shoes there, coat there, umbrella there — turns the exit into a checklist the child can run without a grown-up chasing.',
  know:'You need the card, one hook (or one clear spot), one basket and the real shoes. The doormat and the key are taught where they actually are: at the actual door.',
  why:'The key carries the safety word: keys are grown-up treasure, high up, never play. Said with the word, every time, and the rule needs no second lesson.',
  dev:'Organization by place (a home for the going-out things), self-dressing skills (shoes on at the mat), and weather-thinking — hat for sun, umbrella for rain.',
  vocab:'key, doormat, umbrella, hat, shoe, door',
  teach:'1) Show the card at the front door: "everything that helps us leave lives HERE."\n2) The doormat: wipe, wipe, wipe — the mat&rsquo;s one job, demonstrated twice, adopted forever.\n3) Shoes at the mat: toes pointing out, ready to slide in — the child sits, puts them on, done.\n4) Hook or basket: hat and umbrella get their home — sun hat here, rain umbrella there.\n5) The key goes high: where it lives, why it lives there, and whose job it is (the grown-up&rsquo;s).',
  say:'"By the door — the launchpad! Shoes at the mat, toes out. Hat on its hook. Umbrella waits for rain. Wipe, wipe, wipe — and we are off!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "By the Door" sit over a golden key, a striped doormat, a folded blue umbrella, a blue hat with a green band and a lace-up sneaker.',
  ask:'"What do we wipe our feet on?" (the doormat) "What waits for a rainy day?" (the umbrella) "Whose job is the key?" (the grown-up&rsquo;s — it lives up high)',
  childSay:'Your child might wipe feet on every mat in town, stage umbrella drills on sunny days, or line up the family&rsquo;s shoes with toes out like a parking lot.',
  real:'Every doorway has this zone — school entrances, swimming pools, grandma&rsquo;s hall. The launchpad habit makes the child the smoothest leaver in any building.',
  play:'Launch Check: before any outing, the child runs the checklist aloud at the real door — shoes (toes out), hat if sunny, umbrella if gray, wipe the mat — and rings an imaginary bell when ready. Countdown to departure, run by the smallest officer.',
  check:'Ask: "Where do the shoes wait?" (by the mat, toes out) "What unlocks the door?" (the key — a grown-up&rsquo;s) "What does the mat do?" (wipes our feet)'},
 'letters-and-parcels':{
  title:'The Letters and Parcels Lesson',one:'Post a real letter — and the mailbox becomes a daily place of ceremony.',
  seoTitle:'Letters and Parcels Lesson for Kids — Letter, Envelope, Stamp, Mailbox (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach letters and parcels — letter, envelope, parcel, stamp and mailbox — by posting one real letter and waiting for the magic.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the mail by running it: one real letter, drawn and addressed by the child, posted with ceremony — and then the best part, the daily check.',
  what:'The mail equipment: the letter with words on paper, the envelope as its coat, the stamp as its ticket, the parcel as the box of surprises, and the mailbox where everything knocks.',
  mean:'Mail is slow magic — you post it, you wait, and someday something comes back. The waiting trains patience; the checking builds a daily ritual; the parcel-package pair rides along in both accents.',
  know:'You need the card, paper, crayons, an envelope, a real stamp and a real mailbox or post box within walking distance. The return address can be grandma — someone will write back.',
  why:'The stamp is the wonder-word: a tiny sticker that is really a ticket for travel. And the arrival rule is gentle but firm — the mail belongs to the family, opened together.',
  dev:'Mark-making with real purpose (a letter someone will read), address awareness (our street, our number), and delayed gratification — the finest kind, with a postal service.',
  vocab:'letter, envelope, parcel, package, stamp, mailbox, post',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "this is how the world knocks on our door — in paper."\n2) Make the letter: the child draws; you write a real address on the envelope together.\n3) The stamp goes on top — its ticket — and the name reads like a spell.\n4) The walk to the mailbox: post it, wave, and the ceremony is complete.\n5) From then on, the daily check is the child&rsquo;s: any post today? Anything with MY name on it?',
  say:'"Letters and parcels! The letter wears its envelope coat, the stamp buys the ride, and the mailbox takes it from there. Now we wait — and waiting for post is the best kind of waiting."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Letters and Parcels" sit over a lined letter, a cream envelope, a taped cardboard parcel, a portrait stamp and a blue mailbox on a post with its flag up.',
  ask:'"What carries the letter to grandma?" (the envelope — and the stamp, and the post) "What is a parcel?" (a box of surprises — a package) "Whose job is opening the mail?" (the family&rsquo;s — together)',
  childSay:'Your child might post drawings to everyone they know, check the mailbox twice before breakfast, or address envelopes to the cat.',
  real:'Post offices, birthday cards, library notices — the mail runs the world quietly. A child who posts a letter has joined a system that works in every country on Earth.',
  play:'Post Office: a shoebox counter, paper, envelopes, sticker stamps — the family posts drawings to each other all afternoon. The delivery walk (room to room, very official) completes the loop.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the letter&rsquo;s coat called?" (the envelope) "What buys the ride?" (the stamp) "Where does everything knock?" (the mailbox)'},
 'outside-the-home':{
  title:'The Outside the Home Lesson',one:'Name the edges of home — and say the balcony-and-gate rules where they belong.',
  seoTitle:'Outside the Home Lesson for Kids — Balcony, Gate, Path, Porch (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach outside-the-home words — balcony, gate, mailbox, path and porch — with the edge rules said kindly, on location.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the in-between places — not quite house, not quite street: the balcony, the gate, the path, the porch — and the two rules that live out there.',
  what:'Between the front door and the world lie the edges of home: the balcony up in the air, the gate that clicks, the mailbox on its post, the path that wanders, the porch that waves.',
  mean:'Edges are where homes say this-far. A child who can name the edges — gate, balcony, path — can also be given their rules precisely, and precise rules hold.',
  know:'You need the card and a slow walk of your own frontage: door to gate to path and back. If your home has a balcony, it gets visited; if not, a window ledge counts.',
  why:'The two edge rules are taught on location, kindly: balconies are for holding a grown-up&rsquo;s hand, and gates click shut behind us. Rules at the edge feel like facts, not lectures.',
  dev:'Spatial mapping (near, far, up, along), boundary understanding, and the porch-sit habit — the calmest watching game a family can own.',
  vocab:'balcony, gate, mailbox, path, porch, outside, edge',
  teach:'1) Show the card at the front door: "the home has edges — let us walk them."\n2) Out the door: is there a porch or step? Stand on it — this is where we wave goodbye.\n3) Follow the path: the path has a job — it shows us the way to the gate.\n4) The gate: open it together, then click it shut behind — the rule, said on the spot.\n5) Balcony (or the high window): look at it from below and say the hand-holding rule — then mailbox, and home again.',
  say:'"Outside the home! The path walks us to the gate, the gate clicks shut behind us, and the porch is where we wave. The edges of home are the best seats for watching the world."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Outside the Home" sit over a blue balcony with railings, a double gate between posts, a blue mailbox, a stepping-stone path and a porch with a small roof.',
  ask:'"What walks us from door to gate?" (the path) "What do we do behind a gate?" (click it shut) "Where do we stand to wave goodbye?" (the porch)',
  childSay:'Your child might click every gate in the neighborhood shut with pride, perform porch waves for departing guests, or point out paths wherever you walk.',
  real:'Parks, schools and libraries all have gates and paths — and the rules travel with the words. A child who clicks gates at home clicks them everywhere.',
  play:'Porch Sit: five minutes on the steps or at the window, naming everything that passes — a bird, a gate, a car, a cloud. Quiet voices, no toys, just watching. The oldest game there is.',
  safety:'Two edge rules, said kindly and on location: balconies and high places mean a grown-up hand, always — and gates click shut behind us. The rules belong to the words and come out with them.'},
 'outdoor-home-words':{
  title:'The Outdoor Home Words Lesson',one:'Meet the green half of home — fence, bench, feeder, flower bed — and feed something.',
  seoTitle:'Outdoor Home Words Lesson for Kids — Fence, Bench, Bird Feeder, Flower Bed (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach outdoor home words — fence, bench, bird feeder, watering can and flower bed — with a refill job and the yard-garden pair.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the living edges of home: the fence that draws the circle, the bench that holds two, the feeder and the flower bed that make the yard a habitat.',
  what:'The outdoor home words: the fence, the bench, the bird feeder, the watering can and the flower bed — the green team that makes outside feel like yours.',
  mean:'A yard (or garden) is a child&rsquo;s first habitat, and these are its landmarks. Naming them turns the outside from a place you play into a place you keep.',
  know:'You need the card and the real outside — any fence counts, any bench, any flower. The bird feeder job works with a feeder, a windowsill tray or a handful of seeds.',
  why:'The refill ritual is the lesson&rsquo;s engine: birds visit the feeder that gets filled, flowers open in the bed that gets watered — and the child keeps both calendars.',
  dev:'Care for habitat (birds, plants), the boundary sense (the fence says this-far), and the observation habit — who visited today? What opened overnight?',
  vocab:'fence, bench, bird feeder, watering can, flower bed, yard, garden',
  teach:'1) Show the card outside: "our outside has its own words — let us meet them."\n2) The fence: walk it from one end to the other — this is the circle of our yard. Some say garden, some say yard: both are this patch.\n3) The bench: sit on it together — two seats, one view.\n4) The feeder refill: seeds in, hung where the birds trust, then step back and shush.\n5) The flower bed and the can: one glug per flower, and tomorrow there will be news.',
  say:'"Outdoor home words! The fence draws our circle, the bench holds two, the feeder feeds the birds and the flower bed drinks. Our outside is a habitat — and we are the keepers."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Outdoor Home Words" sit over a pale picket fence, a wooden bench with blue trim, a hanging bird feeder with seeds, a blue watering can and a round flower bed with blooms.',
  ask:'"What draws the circle of the yard?" (the fence) "What do we fill for the birds?" (the bird feeder) "What holds two people?" (the bench)',
  childSay:'Your child might keep a mental list of every bird that visited, water the fence (it does not mind), or sit the whole family on the bench one by one to test it.',
  real:'Parks and school yards have the same landmarks — fences, benches, feeders, beds. The child who knows them reads every outdoor space like a map.',
  play:'Who Came Today: each morning, one look at the feeder and one entry in the bird notebook — a drawing, a tally, a story. By week&rsquo;s end the child knows their yard&rsquo;s regulars by heart.',
  check:'Ask: "What does the fence say?" (this far — the circle of the yard) "What holds the seeds?" (the bird feeder) "Where do the flowers live?" (the flower bed)'},
 'pets-at-home':{
  title:'The Pets at Home Lesson',one:'Gentle hands, quiet voices, pet jobs sized small — the golden rule runs the lesson.',
  seoTitle:'Pets at Home Lesson for Kids — Cat, Dog, Rabbit, Fish Bowl (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach pets-at-home words — cat, dog, fish bowl, rabbit and pet bed — with gentle-hands practice and pet jobs sized for small helpers.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the pets and their manners: the golden rule first — gentle, quiet, let them sleep — then the words, then the jobs.',
  what:'The pets at home: the cat, the dog, the fish bowl, the rabbit and the pet bed — the family members with fur and fins.',
  mean:'Pets are the first someone-smaller-than-you a child learns to care for. The rule order matters: gentle before naming, naming before jobs — the respect is load-bearing.',
  know:'You need the card and any pets in reach — your own, a neighbor&rsquo;s, or the card alone. The golden rule is rehearsed before any paw is touched: gentle, quiet, let them sleep.',
  why:'The pet bed rule looks small and is not: the pet&rsquo;s place is the pet&rsquo;s, like the child&rsquo;s bed is the child&rsquo;s. Respect for territory scales up to siblings and friends.',
  dev:'Gentle-touch practice (one finger stroke), reading animal signals (tail tells the truth), and care routines — food scoop, fresh water — sized to be genuinely helpful.',
  vocab:'cat, dog, fish bowl, rabbit, pet bed, gentle',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "the smallest family members — with the biggest characters."\n2) The golden rule, said together: gentle hands, quiet voices, we let pets sleep.\n3) Practice the one-finger stroke on your own arm — that is pet-touch size.\n4) Meet the pets by name: the card&rsquo;s cat, dog, rabbit, fish — and the real ones if you have them, each with their word.\n5) One pet job, sized small: the food scoop or the water bowl — done once, done gently, done again tomorrow.',
  say:'"Pets at home! The cat naps, the dog wags, the fish swims circles. Our rule comes first: gentle hands, quiet voices, let them sleep. Then — the food scoop. That is your job now."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Pets at Home" sit over a striped cat sitting tall, a spotted dog with a wagging tail, a goldfish in a round fish bowl with a plant, a cream rabbit and a pet bed with a paw print.',
  ask:'"What is the golden rule of pets?" (gentle, quiet, let them sleep) "Where does the fish live?" (the fish bowl) "Whose place is the pet bed?" (the pet&rsquo;s)',
  childSay:'Your child might shush the whole house for a napping cat, scoop pet food with enormous ceremony, or explain the golden rule to visitors at the door.',
  real:'Every pet you meet wears the same rule — friends&rsquo; dogs, farm cats, class rabbits. The child who learned gentle-hands at home is trusted with every animal after.',
  play:'Vet Practice: teddy is the patient; the child runs the gentle check — one-finger stroke, quiet voice, let him rest, food and water nearby. Then swap: the child is the pet, you are the gentle vet. Both sides learn the rule.',
  check:'Ask: "How do we touch a pet?" (one finger, gentle) "What do we do when the pet sleeps?" (let them sleep) "Whose place is the pet bed?" (the pet&rsquo;s own)'},
 'sounds-at-home':{
  title:'The Sounds at Home Lesson',one:'One quiet minute: the house&rsquo;s voices appear — and each one means something.',
  seoTitle:'Sounds at Home Lesson for Kids — Doorbell, Telephone, Radio, Alarm (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach sounds at home — doorbell, telephone, headphones, radio and alarm — with a listen-hunt and the rule for each sound.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the house&rsquo;s voices by listening for them: one quiet minute, a count of sounds, and a rule attached to the two that matter most.',
  what:'The home speaks in sounds: the doorbell announces, the telephone rings, the radio sings, the headphones hush, the alarm wakes.',
  mean:'Sound words carry rules — doorbell means come together, alarm means up we get — and rules attached to sounds are the fastest ones children learn. The ears do the teaching.',
  know:'You need the card and one quiet minute. The listen-hunt needs nothing else: the house is already performing. If the doorbell never rings, ring it yourself — once, by arrangement.',
  why:'The doorbell rule is safety and manners in one: a visitor is met with a grown-up, every time. Said calmly with the word, it never needs to be said in a panic.',
  dev:'Auditory discrimination (which sound is which), sound-meaning mapping, and the quiet-minute discipline that makes listening a skill rather than an accident.',
  vocab:'doorbell, telephone, headphones, radio, alarm, sound',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "our house has five voices — let us hear them."\n2) The quiet minute: sit, close eyes half-way, count the sounds you hear on your fingers.\n3) Match what you heard to the card — a hum, a tick, a car outside.\n4) The two big voices: the doorbell (who is it? we go together) and the alarm (time to wake).\n5) The headphone rule: music just for you — quiet ears, quiet room, everyone happy.',
  say:'"Sounds at home — listen! Ding-dong means a visitor: we go to the door TOGETHER. The alarm means morning. What is the house saying right now?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Sounds at Home" sit over a doorbell chime with a bell mark, a push-button telephone, blue headphones, a transistor radio with its dial and an alarm clock with twin bells.',
  ask:'"What does ding-dong mean?" (a visitor — we go together) "What wakes us up?" (the alarm) "What keeps music just for you?" (the headphones)',
  childSay:'Your child might run to the door for every imaginary ding-dong, hold the headphones to your ears to share, or sleep-test the alarm with deep suspicion.',
  real:'Every building speaks — hotels, grandma&rsquo;s, holiday lets. The child who knows the doorbell rule greets every strange doorbell exactly the same way: with a grown-up.',
  play:'Sound Detective: the child closes their eyes; you make one house sound — tap the door (knock knock), ring a bell, hum the radio. The detective names it and points at the card. Five rounds, one sharp-eared winner.',
  check:'Ask: "Who goes to the doorbell?" (everyone — with a grown-up) "What sings in the kitchen?" (the radio) "What keeps the music just for one?" (the headphones)'},
 'sounds-around-home':{
  title:'The Sounds Around Home Lesson',one:'The house plays its own music — footsteps, creaks, drips — and your child learns to read it.',
  seoTitle:'Sounds Around Home Lesson for Kids — Footsteps, Creaky Door, Running Tap (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach the sounds around home — footsteps, creaky door, ticking clock, running tap, ringing phone — and what each sound is telling you.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the house&rsquo;s self-made soundtrack: the sounds nobody switches on, from footsteps to creaks to drips — and the news each one carries.',
  what:'The sounds around home: footsteps tip-tapping, the door&rsquo;s creak, the clock&rsquo;s tick, the tap&rsquo;s running (the faucet, across the ocean), the phone&rsquo;s ring.',
  mean:'Sound-reading is a safety skill wearing a game&rsquo;s clothes: footsteps say who is coming, a running tap says somebody left it on, a creak says which door. Ears that read the house are useful ears.',
  know:'You need the card and the performing house. Every item can also be performed by body: march the footsteps, groan the creak, tick-tock the tongue, drip the fingers.',
  why:'The tap-faucet pair returns here in its splashiest form: a running tap is the house&rsquo;s loudest news. And the body-performance games make the words physical.',
  dev:'Sound-source reasoning (what made THAT?), onomatopoeia play (tip-tap, creak, tick-tock), and the detective habit of asking what the sound means.',
  vocab:'footsteps, creaky door, ticking clock, running tap, ringing phone',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "the house plays music all day — here is the band."\n2) Perform each sound with your body: tip-tap feet, creeeak voice, tick-tock tongue, drip-drip fingers, ring-ring hands.\n3) Listen for real: which band member is playing right now? The clock is almost certainly on stage.\n4) The news game: what does a running tap say? (turn me off!) What do footsteps say? (someone is coming!)\n5) The tap check: is any tap dripping in the house? One walk, one listening tour, one hero.',
  say:'"Sounds around home! Tip-tap, creak, tick-tock, drip-drip, ring-ring — the house talks all day. What is it telling us right now?"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Sounds Around Home" sit over two blue footprints, an open door with motion lines by its hinge, a round ticking clock, a tap with a drop falling and a vintage phone mid-ring.',
  ask:'"What do footsteps tell us?" (someone is coming) "What does a running tap say?" (turn me off) "What says tick-tock all night?" (the clock)',
  childSay:'Your child might announce every creak in the building, patrol the house for dripping taps like a plumber-in-chief, or perform the whole band on request.',
  real:'The listening habit lasts: a child who reads house sounds reads street sounds, classroom sounds, nature sounds. Ears trained at home stay trained.',
  play:'House Orchestra: the family performs the five sounds in a loop — footsteps, creak, tick-tock, drip, ring — faster and faster until it collapses into giggles. Then the quiet-minute hunt for the real versions.',
  check:'Ask: "What does creeeak mean?" (a door is opening) "What is the tap saying when it runs?" (turn me off) "Who makes tip-tap?" (the footsteps — someone walking)'},
 'technology-at-home':{
  title:'The Technology at Home Lesson',one:'Name the glowing machines — and let the child run the one real job: charger duty.',
  seoTitle:'Technology at Home Lesson for Kids — Computer, Tablet, Charger, Camera (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach technology at home — computer, tablet, headphones, charger and camera — with charger duty as the child&rsquo;s first real tech job.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the glowing family by their jobs: the computer works, the tablet taps, the camera clicks, the headphones hush — and the charger, the child&rsquo;s own job, feeds them all.',
  what:'Technology at home: the computer on its desk, the tablet in small hands, the headphones, the charger with its cable, the camera that says cheese.',
  mean:'Machines that get fed are machines that work — and feeding them is a job a four-year-old can run perfectly. Ownership of one real tech job beats a hundred screen rules.',
  know:'You need the card and the real machines. The charger job needs a reachable plug and a bedtime slot; the camera gets one supervised click; the rules come with the words.',
  why:'The tech manners ride with the names: tablets live where everyone can see, cameras ask before they click, screens sleep in the kitchen at night. Said calmly with the words, they hold.',
  dev:'Machine-care responsibility (charging is real maintenance), tech vocabulary, and the ask-before-clicking etiquette that scales to every camera the child will ever meet.',
  vocab:'computer, tablet, headphones, charger, camera, screen',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "the glowing machines — every one has a job."\n2) Meet them by job: the computer works, the tablet plays and learns, the camera remembers, the headphones hush.\n3) Charger duty, installed: at bedtime the child finds the tablet, brings it to the plug, and the grown-up connects — feeding time announced.\n4) Camera etiquette: one supervised photo — and the rule, cameras ask before they click.\n5) The sleeping rule: screens sleep in the kitchen at night — said like a fact about machines, which it is.',
  say:'"Technology at home! The computer works, the tablet taps, the camera says cheese — and you, you are the charger. Machines that get fed at night work in the morning."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Technology at Home" sit over a desktop computer with keyboard and mouse, a tablet standing upright, blue headphones, a charger plug with its coiled cable and a compact camera.',
  ask:'"Which machine works at the desk?" (the computer) "What do machines eat?" (their power — from the charger) "What does a camera do before it clicks?" (it asks)',
  childSay:'Your child might tuck the tablet in beside the charger like a teddy, narrate feeding time to the whole house, or demand the camera ask permission of everything.',
  real:'Every home runs on these machines — and on the small rituals that keep them alive. A child with charger duty understands power, plugs and patience before any screen-time debate begins.',
  play:'Tech Care Station: set up a bedtime charging shelf — one plug, one spot, one job. The child runs the whole routine: find, carry, plug (with you), announce. The machines sleep; the job is real.',
  safety:'Plugs and wall sockets are grown-up hands: the child finds and carries, the grown-up connects. Screens live in family rooms, not bedrooms — said with the words, every night.'},
 'safe-at-home':{
  title:'The Safe at Home Lesson',one:'Visit the quiet guardians — the first-aid box, the helmet, the handwash — and learn where safety lives.',
  seoTitle:'Safe at Home Lesson for Kids — First-Aid Box, Helmet, Keys (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach safe-at-home words — first-aid box, helmet, key, light switch and handwash — on a gentle safety tour of your own house.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches safety as geography: the child learns where the safe things live, because knowing where safety lives is the first skill of being safe.',
  what:'The safe-at-home team: the first-aid box for bumps, the helmet for wheels, the key up high, the light switch, and the handwash at every sink.',
  mean:'Safety tours done calmly make safety ordinary. A child who has peeked inside the first-aid box on a normal Tuesday is not frightened of it on the day it is needed.',
  know:'You need the card and the real locations: the first-aid box, the helmet, the sinks. Nothing is opened in an emergency mood — this is a tour, not a drill.',
  why:'The kit-box pair rides here too — box in the UK, kit in the US, the same brave little container. And the keys rule comes attached: grown-up treasure, up high, never play.',
  dev:'Location memory (where does safety live?), hygiene habits (handwash at every sink), and the calm-fact tone that keeps safety from becoming scary.',
  vocab:'first-aid box, first-aid kit, helmet, key, light switch, handwash',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "our home has guardians — let us visit them all."\n2) The first-aid box: open it together — plasters, cream, the family phone list. Say where it lives and put it back.\n3) The helmet: try it on — helmets go on before wheels, bikes and scooters alike.\n4) The handwash: one pump at the real sink — where it lives, when we use it.\n5) The light switch and the key: lights off when we leave a room; keys up high, grown-up treasure.',
  say:'"Safe at home — the guardians! The first-aid box waits for bumps, the helmet waits for wheels, the handwash waits at every sink. You know where safety lives now."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Safe at Home" sit over a first-aid box with a red cross, a cycling helmet, a single key, a light switch on its plate and a round green handwash bottle.',
  ask:'"Where does the first-aid box live?" (say your real spot) "When does the helmet go on?" (before wheels) "Whose job is the key?" (the grown-ups&rsquo; — up high)',
  childSay:'Your child might parade in the helmet all afternoon, restock the first-aid box like a nurse, or switch off every light in empty rooms with great authority.',
  real:'The same guardians stand watch in every home you visit — ask your hosts where the first-aid box lives. Knowing to ask is half the lesson already learned.',
  play:'Guardian Tour: draw the house as a simple map, and the child marks X on each guardian&rsquo;s home — first-aid box, helmet, handwash sinks. The map goes on the fridge; safety has addresses now.',
  check:'Ask: "What do we put on before wheels?" (the helmet) "Where does the key stay?" (up high — grown-up treasure) "What do we do at the sink?" (handwash)'},
 'home-safety':{
  title:'The Home Safety Lesson',one:'Meet the protectors — smoke alarm, torch, safety gate — so a beep is a friend, never a fright.',
  seoTitle:'Home Safety Lesson for Kids — Smoke Alarm, Torch, Safety Gate (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach home safety — smoke alarm, torch (flashlight), safety gate, fire extinguisher and reflective vest — with a torch test and a meeting-spot plan.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the machines and habits that guard the house — before any of them are ever needed, so the loud ones are already friends.',
  what:'The protectors: the smoke alarm with its loud voice, the torch (the flashlight) for dark corners, the safety gate on the stairs, the fire extinguisher and reflective vest for the grown-ups.',
  mean:'A smoke alarm is a friend with a loud voice — said long before it ever beeps for real. Children who know the machines are startled by nothing and helped by all of it.',
  know:'You need the card and the real protectors: find the alarm (do not test the sound with a small child in the room), test the torch button, point at the gate.',
  why:'The torch-flashlight pair lives right on the card, and the meeting-spot plan is the lesson&rsquo;s quietly serious half: stop, go outside, meet at the tree — rehearsed as a game, known as a plan.',
  dev:'Emergency-machine familiarity (loud is warning, not danger), a family meeting plan, and the grown-up-tools category said warmly: grown-ups use, children know.',
  vocab:'smoke alarm, torch, flashlight, safety gate, fire extinguisher, reflective vest',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "these five guard our house — let us meet them all."\n2) The smoke alarm: point at the real one — a friend with a loud voice. If it beeps, we go outside. That is its whole job.\n3) The torch: test the button in a dim corner — the child drives the beam. Flashlight, torch: both names, one light.\n4) The safety gate: find it, walk to it, say what it keeps little ones safe from.\n5) The plan, rehearsed once: if the alarm says beep-beep-beep — stop, go outside, meet at the tree. Then the extinguisher and vest: grown-up tools, child-known.',
  say:'"Home safety! The smoke alarm is a friend with a loud voice — if it beeps, we go outside and meet at the tree. The torch lights the dark. You know the plan."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Home Safety" sit over a round ceiling smoke alarm, a blue torch, a stair safety gate, a red fire extinguisher and a hi-vis reflective vest.',
  ask:'"What does the smoke alarm say when it works?" (beep — loud — we go outside) "What lights the dark?" (the torch — the flashlight) "Where do we meet outside?" (say your real spot)',
  childSay:'Your child might rehearse the meeting-spot march for fun, guard the torch like a lighthouse keeper, or explain the loud friend to every visitor.',
  real:'Hotels and grandparent houses have alarms and exits too — the child who knows the plan at home looks for the meeting spot everywhere. That habit is worth carrying.',
  play:'Meeting-Tree Drill: one sunny day, walk the plan as a game — from the bedroom, out the door, to the tree, everyone counted. Repeat any season, any mood. The drill is the comfort.',
  safety:'The extinguisher and the vest are grown-up tools — said with the words, every time. The alarm gets pointed at, never tested with small ears in the room, and the plan is rehearsed in daylight, calmly.'},
 'water-and-energy':{
  title:'The Water and Energy Lesson',one:'Meet the home&rsquo;s two treasures — and appoint the energy police for one proud day.',
  seoTitle:'Water and Energy Lesson for Kids — Tap, Light Bulb, Plug, Solar Panel (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach water and energy — tap, light bulb, plug, water bottle and solar panel — with the energy-police game that makes saving a habit.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches where the house gets its power and its water — and turns saving both into a job with a badge, not a scolding.',
  what:'Water and energy: the tap that brings water, the light bulb that glows, the plug that feeds the machines, the water bottle that travels, the solar panel that catches sunshine.',
  mean:'Children are magnificent absolutists — the tap is RUNNING! — and that exact energy is what saving runs on. The lesson aims it at two treasures and one proud day of patrolling.',
  know:'You need the card and the real utilities: a tap, a light switch, a plug (observed only). The solar panel may be on your roof, a neighbor&rsquo;s, or only in books — all three count.',
  why:'The plug rule is the safety spine of the card — plugs are grown-up hands — and it rides along with the fun instead of arriving as a lecture. The tap-faucet pair returns once more.',
  dev:'Resource awareness (where does it come from? where does it go?), habit-building (tap off while brushing), and the plug boundary said as fact.',
  vocab:'tap, faucet, light bulb, plug, water bottle, solar panel, energy',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "our house drinks and glows — here is how."\n2) The tap: run it, count two seconds, off — and the brushing rule: tap off while teeth get brushed.\n3) The light bulb: switch one on, one off — lights for rooms we are IN.\n4) The plug: look with eyes only — the wall feeds the machines, and the wall side is a grown-up&rsquo;s hands.\n5) The solar panel and the bottle: sunshine becomes power; the bottle refills again and again — the refillable habit.',
  say:'"Water and energy! The tap brings the drink, the bulb brings the light — and both are treasures. Tap off while brushing, lights off when we leave. You are the energy police today."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Water and Energy" sit over a tap with one drop falling, a glowing light bulb, a two-pin plug, a labeled water bottle and a tilted solar panel.',
  ask:'"What brings the water?" (the tap — the faucet) "What should be off while we brush?" (the tap) "Whose hands touch the plug?" (the grown-up&rsquo;s)',
  childSay:'Your child might police every light in the postcode, time the tooth-brushing tap with real rigor, or ask whether the sun can charge the tablet.',
  real:'Utilities behave the same everywhere — hotels, holidays, school. The child who turns taps off at home does it in every bathroom they ever visit.',
  play:'Energy Police: one day on duty — a paper badge, a notebook of finds (light on in empty room! tap left running!) and one official report at dinner. Off duty tomorrow; the habits stay on.',
  safety:'Plugs and wall sockets are grown-up territory — eyes yes, hands no. Said as fact with the word, every time: the wall side belongs to grown-up hands.'},
 'recycling-words':{
  title:'The Recycling Words Lesson',one:'Set up the two-bin game — and sorting becomes a sport the child referees.',
  seoTitle:'Recycling Lesson for Kids — Glass, Can, Cardboard, Recycling Bin (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach recycling words — glass bottle, can, cardboard box, food scraps and recycling bin — with a two-bin sorting game that sticks.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches recycling as a sorting sport: two bins, a stream of empties, and a child who learns that things do not end — they go around again.',
  what:'The recycling words: the glass bottle, the can, the cardboard box, the food scraps and the recycling bin — the materials and the home they go back to.',
  mean:'Sorting is a two-year-old skill wearing a green badge. Name the materials — glass, metal, paper, food — and the child sorts forever, everywhere, with a referee&rsquo;s joy.',
  know:'You need the card, two labeled bins (recycling and rubbish) and a starter stream of clean empties. The compost corner can be real or imaginary — the food scraps need a home either way.',
  why:'The compost is the wonder-word: food scraps become soil. Even explained simply, the loop — banana to soil to plant — is the deepest magic this set offers.',
  dev:'Material recognition (glass, metal, paper, food), sorting as a category skill, and the loop thinking that makes recycling feel like a fact of life rather than a rule.',
  vocab:'glass bottle, can, cardboard box, food scraps, recycling bin, compost',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "things do not end — they go around. Here is how."\n2) Set the bins: recycling and rubbish, labeled by the child, placed side by side.\n3) Sort a starter stream together, naming materials: glass bottle — clink — recycling. Can — recycling. Cardboard — recycling. Banana peel — food scraps.\n4) The compost story, simply: the scraps go to the worms, the worms make soil, the soil grows more food.\n5) The child referees from now on — every empty gets dunked with ceremony.',
  say:'"Recycling words! The bottle, the can and the box go around again. The scraps feed the worms, the worms make the soil. Sorting game — on!"',
  notice:'On this card the words "Recycling Words" sit over a clear glass bottle, an unopened can, an open cardboard box, an apple core and banana peel, and a blue recycling bin with the loop arrows.',
  ask:'"Where does the glass bottle go?" (the recycling bin) "Where do the banana peels go?" (the food scraps — the compost) "What do the worms make?" (the soil)',
  childSay:'Your child might interrogate every wrapper in the house, referee the grandparents&rsquo; sorting on visits, or guard the compost with quiet pride.',
  real:'Recycling runs in schools, parks and every town — bins with colors and rules. A child who sorts at home reads every public bin correctly, in any country.',
  play:'Bin Slam: hold up a clean empty — the child calls the material (glass! metal! paper! food!) and slam-dunks the right bin. Ten items, ten dunks, one very loud victory lap.',
  check:'Ask: "What is the bottle made of?" (glass) "Where do the scraps go?" (the compost — the worms) "What do the arrows on the bin mean?" (it goes around again)'},
 'care-for-earth':{
  title:'The Care for Earth Lesson',one:'Five planet-sized ideas, sized for one small daily job — and the badge is real.',
  seoTitle:'Care for Earth Lesson for Kids — Save Energy, Save Water, Recycle, Reuse (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach caring for the Earth — saving energy and water, recycling, reusing — with one Earth job per week that actually sticks.',
  intro:'This lesson gathers the green ideas into one card — save energy, save water, recycle, reuse, care for Earth — and shrinks them to the only size that works: one job, one week, done daily.',
  what:'Care for Earth is the family of green habits: saving energy, saving water, recycling what we use, reusing what we can — all of it looking after the one planet everyone shares.',
  mean:'One job done daily beats ten jobs done never. The lesson&rsquo;s whole craft is the shrink: the whole Earth is too big for a four-year-old, but one light switch is exactly the right size.',
  know:'You need the card, a paper badge and one chosen job for the week — the light-switch patrol, the tap-timer or the reuse-bag carrier.',
  why:'Reuse is the newest word here: the bag used again, the jar refilled, the box turned into a garage. The idea lands fastest through the family&rsquo;s own habits, caught rather than taught.',
  dev:'Habit formation (one job, one week, daily), cause-and-effect care (lights off means power saved), and planet-scale belonging — my home is inside a bigger home.',
  vocab:'save energy, save water, recycle, reuse, care for Earth, planet',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "Earth is the biggest home there is — here is how we care for it."\n2) Read the five ideas with their gestures: lights off (fingers flick), tap off (wrist turn), recycle (loop), reuse (hug the bag), care for Earth (hands around the heart).\n3) Choose one Earth job for the week — the child chooses; the choosing is the buy-in.\n4) Make the badge: the job written on it, worn for the week, retired with honor.\n5) Friday review: what did we save? Numbers optional — pride mandatory.',
  say:'"Care for Earth — the biggest home of all! Lights off when we leave, tap off while we brush, bottles go around, bags get reused. Pick your job: the planet is hiring."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Care for Earth" sit over a light bulb marked save energy, a tap marked save water, a recycling bin marked recycle, a refillable bottle marked reuse and a small tree marked care for Earth.',
  ask:'"What do we switch off when we leave a room?" (the light) "What gets reused?" (the bags — the jars — the boxes) "Whose job is the planet?" (everyone&rsquo;s — starting with yours)',
  childSay:'Your child might appoint themselves permanently to the light patrol, reuse every box into a toy garage, or comfort the tap personally each morning.',
  real:'Earth-care habits travel: park bins, school lights, the family car. A child with one green job at home notices every wasteful light in every building they enter — forever, useful.',
  play:'Earth Job Wheel: five jobs on a paper wheel — lights, tap, recycling, bags, plant-watering — spin it each Sunday. The wheel picks; the child performs; the planet quietly wins.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the two treasures we save?" (water and energy) "What does reuse mean?" (use it again) "Who is the Earth job for this week?" (say the name proudly)'},
 'plants-at-home':{
  title:'The Plants at Home Lesson',one:'Adopt one plant, name it, water it — and the green card becomes a green habit.',
  seoTitle:'Plants at Home Lesson for Kids — Plant, Leaf, Flower, Tree, Seed (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 3–5: how to teach plants at home — plant, leaf, flower, tree and seed — by adopting and naming one real houseplant as the child&rsquo;s own.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the plant words through adoption: one real plant, one name, one small watering cup — and the whole vocabulary grows from there.',
  what:'The plants at home: the plant in its pot, the leaf that catches light, the flower that shows color, the tree that shades the yard, and the seed where every one of them began.',
  mean:'A named plant is a watered plant — Ferdie the fern gets his cup twice as faithfully as an unnamed one. Ownership, not instruction, is what makes the habit grow.',
  know:'You need the card, one houseplant (any survivor will do), a small cup for watering and a name, chosen with ceremony. The windowsill bean jar is the bonus experiment.',
  why:'The seed is the hinge word: something fingernail-small becomes a tree. One bean in a jar on the windowsill proves it in two weeks — the set&rsquo;s most reliable miracle.',
  dev:'Care routines for living things, observation over time (new leaf? new flower?), and the plant-body map — roots below, stem up, leaves out.',
  vocab:'plant, leaf, flower, tree, seed, pot, grow',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "our home has green residents — here are their words."\n2) Meet the parts on the card: the leaf catches the light, the flower shows its colors, the tree shades the yard.\n3) The seed story: every plant on this card started as a seed the size of a fingernail.\n4) The adoption: choose the plant, choose the name, hand over the watering cup — once a week, small drink.\n5) The bean jar to finish: bean, wet cotton, windowsill — and the watching begins.',
  say:'"Plants at home! The leaf catches the light, the flower shows its colors — and every one of them started as a seed. Your plant has a name now; the cup is yours."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Plants at Home" sit over a potted plant with wide leaves, a single green leaf, a white flower with a blue center, a round-crowned tree and one teardrop seed.',
  ask:'"What catches the light for the plant?" (the leaf) "What was every plant first?" (a seed) "Whose job is the watering cup?" (say the name — the adopter&rsquo;s)',
  childSay:'Your child might greet the plant by name each morning, measure the bean jar hourly, or assign leaves to the whole family as badges.',
  real:'Plants wait in every grandma&rsquo;s window, every classroom, every park. A child who knows plant words and plant care is welcomed as a helper in all of them.',
  play:'Plant Diary: one page per week — the child draws their plant exactly as it is today. New leaf? New pot? The diary catches it all, and the habit of looking closely grows with the plant.',
  check:'Ask: "Where does every plant begin?" (a seed) "What part shows the colors?" (the flower) "Who waters Ferdie?" (the child — once a week, small cup)'},
 'caring-for-plants':{
  title:'The Caring for Plants Lesson',one:'Plant one seed in one pot: the child&rsquo;s seed, the child&rsquo;s job, the child&rsquo;s sprout.',
  seoTitle:'Caring for Plants Lesson for Kids — Pot, Seed, Soil, Watering Can (Home and Daily Life)',
  metaDescription:'A step-by-step Home and Daily Life lesson for ages 4–5: how to teach caring for plants — plant pot, seed, soil, watering can and sunlight — with one real seed planted and raised by the child.',
  intro:'This lesson teaches the five things every plant needs — pot, soil, seed, water, sun — by planting one real seed that belongs entirely to the child.',
  what:'Caring for plants means giving them what they need: the pot to live in, the soil to root in, the seed to start from, the watering can to drink from, the sunlight to eat.',
  mean:'A planted seed is a responsibility with a heartbeat — slow, forgiving, and entirely dependent on the child showing up. Miss a day, water twice; the plant forgives, and the habit forms.',
  know:'You need the card, one small pot, seed compost, one big seed (a bean or sunflower — both forgiving), a small watering can and the brightest windowsill in the house.',
  why:'The crayon-line height chart is the lesson&rsquo;s celebration: when the sprout appears, its height is marked and dated. Growing things get measured; pride gets recorded.',
  dev:'Sequencing a living process (pot, soil, seed, water, sun), daily care with real stakes, and patience — the slowest virtue, taught by the fastest forgiving teacher.',
  vocab:'plant pot, seed, soil, watering can, sunlight, sprout',
  teach:'1) Show the card: "five things every plant needs — we have all five."\n2) Pot first: the child names it (their pot, their choice) and fills it with soil — hands in the dirt, that is the lesson signing on.\n3) The seed: one big seed, one finger-deep hole, tucked in with a pat.\n4) Water and sun: one small glug from the can — then the brightest sill, and the promise: we check every day.\n5) The waiting, done right: one check each morning, a small drink when the soil is dry — and when the sprout comes, the crayon line and the date.',
  say:'"Caring for plants! Soil in the pot, seed tucked in, one small drink, the sunniest window. Now the best part — every morning, we look. One day soon: a sprout, and it is YOURS."',
  notice:'On this card the words "Caring for Plants" sit over a terracotta plant pot, one fat seed, a mound of dark soil, a blue watering can mid-pour and a bright sun with rays.',
  ask:'"What does the seed tuck into?" (the soil) "What does the plant drink?" (water from the can) "What does the plant eat?" (the sunlight)',
  childSay:'Your child might check the pot before breakfast in pajamas, water it with tea-party precision, or draw the sprout taller than it is — with artistic license and total joy.',
  real:'Gardens, classrooms and community plots all run this sequence — pot, soil, seed, water, sun. A child who has raised one sprout is a gardener wherever a pot is offered.',
  play:'Sprout Height Chart: when the sprout appears, mark its height with a crayon line and the date — then mark again each week. The chart becomes the family&rsquo;s slowest, proudest graph.',
  check:'Ask: "What are the five things a plant needs?" (pot, soil, seed, water, sun) "How often do we check?" (every morning) "Whose sprout is it?" (say the name — the grower&rsquo;s)'}
};
